\subsection{Study of the deblurring problem}

The degradation operator $\A$ of the deblurring inverse problem is a channelwise convolution by a certain blur kernel $\bc \in \R^{\OMN}$. In the following, we denote by $\bC$, the block diagonal RGB convolution for which $\bC_{\bc}$ is on each block and where $\bc \in \R^{\OMN}$ is a blur kernel. In other terms, $\bC$ applies the same convolution by the blur kernel $\bc$ on each channel of an image. We focus on three blur kernels: the zoom-out bicubic kernel with a factor $r=16$, which is the convolution part of the super-resolution (SR) problem and two motion blur kernels, generated by an online code\footnote{\url{https://github.com/LeviBorodenko/motionblur}}, which is also used in \cite{Chung_DPS_ICLR_2023}. The effect of these degradations is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:blurred_images}.


