\subsection{Numerical study of the deblurring problem: Exact computation of the 2-Wasserstein distance}

We observe the exact 2-Wasserstein distances between the forward process and the backward processes generated by the different algorithms DPS, $\Pi$GDM, CGDM for three different blur kernels examples with measurement noise level $\sigma = 10/255$, as illustrated in \Cref{fig:W2_SR16,fig:W2_blur1,fig:W2_blur6}. We use the exact unconditional score in this context (\Cref{eq:Gaussian_score_DDPM}) and the different algorithm expression of \Cref{tab:comparison_expression_grad_cond}.
\emile{To emphasize the differences between the algorithms, we do not plot exactly the complete 2-Wasserstein distance between the distributions.} We note that the 2-Wasserstein formula for simultaneously diagonalizable covariance matrices (\Cref{eq:W2_Gaussian_diagonalizable}) can be decomposed into two components. Denote by $\left(\lambda_{i,\bSigma}\right){1 \leq i \leq d}$ the eigenvalues of $\bSigma$, where the null eigenvalues correspond to the kernel of $\bSigma$. We denote by $\PkerS$ the orthogonal projection onto $\ker \bSigma$. For another matrix $\bSigma_{2}$ that is diagonalizable in the same orthogonal basis, with eigenvalues $(\lambda_{i,2})_{1\leq  d}$,
\begin{align}
\footnotesize
	& \Wass^2\left(\mathcal{N}(\bmu,\bSigma),\mathcal{N}(\bmu_2,\bSigma_2)\right) \\
	 = & \|\bmu-\bmu_2\|^2+ \sum_{1\leq i \leq d}(\sqrt{\lambda}_{i,\bSigma}-\sqrt{\lambda}_{i,2})^2 \\
	 = & \footnotesize\underbrace{\|\PkerS(\bmu-\bmu_2)\|^2  + \sum_{\lambda_{\bSigma,i} = 0}(\sqrt{\lambda}_{i,2})^2}_{:=\WasskerS^2\left(\mathcal{N}(\bmu,\bSigma),\mathcal{N}(\bmu_2,\bSigma_2)\right)} \\
	 & +\footnotesize \underbrace{\|(\I-\PkerS)(\bmu-\bmu_2)\|^2+\sum_{\lambda_{\bSigma,i} \neq 0}(\sqrt{\lambda}_{i,\bSigma}-\sqrt{\lambda}_{2,i})^2}_{:=\WassorthokerS^2\left(\mathcal{N}(\bmu,\bSigma),\mathcal{N}(\bmu_2,\bSigma_2)\right)}. 
\end{align}
% The more informative part of this 2-Wasserstein error is the component on $(\ker \bSigma)^\perp$ that describes the generated samples.
In \Cref{fig:W2_SR16,fig:W2_blur1,fig:W2_blur6}, we plot $\WassorthokerS$. \emile{ Let us discuss why this metric is of particular interest by showing that $\WasskerS$ is identical across all algorithms.} Indeed, on $\ker \bSigma$, we can precisely describe the iterations of the different algorithms. All algorithms behave identically on the zero eigenvalues of $\bSigma$, acting like an unconditional DDPM within this kernel subspace. This can be seen by considering the operators $\A_t$ and vectors $\bb_t$ defined in \Cref{sec:recursive_computation_backward_distributions}. For $1 \leq t \leq T$,
{\small
\begin{align}
	 \PkerS\A_t % \\ \notag 
%	& = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}(\PkerS -\beta_t \PkerS\bSigma_t^{-1} -\beta_t \overline{\alpha}_t\PkerS\bSigma_t^{-1}\bSigma \A^T\left(\bC^\text{algo}_{\V \mid t}\right)^{-1}\A\bSigma\bSigma_t^{-1}) \\
	& = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\left(\PkerS -\beta_t \PkerS\bSigma_t^{-1}\right) \\
	& = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\PkerS\left(\I -\beta_t \frac{1}{1-\alphabar_t}\I\right), \\
	\PkerS \bb_t
	 %& =  \beta_t\sqrt{\overline{\alpha}}_{t-1}\PkerS\bSigma_t^{-1}\bSigma \A^T\left(\bC_{\V \mid t}\right)^{-1}(\V-\A\bmu+\alphabar_t\A\bSigma\bSigma_t^{-1}\bmu) \\
    &\quad + \frac{\beta_t}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\PkerS\bSigma_t^{-1}\bmu \\
	& = \PkerS\frac{\beta_t}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\frac{1}{1-\alphabar_t}\bmu.
\end{align} 
}
\emile{These expressions are independent of $\bC_{\V \mid t}$, the covariance matrix that distinguishes the different algorithms. As a consequence, the 2-Wasserstein on $\ker\bSigma$ error is}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
	& \WasskerS^2\left(\mathcal{N}(\bmu,\bSigma),\mathcal{N}(\bmu_{2,t},\bSigma_{2,t})\right)\\
	& := \|\PkerS(\bmu-\bmu_{2,t})\|^2+ (\dim \ker)\times \mathcal{E}_0(t)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{E}_0(t)$ is the error provided by the DDPM algorithm, with an exact score on zero eigenvalues at time $t$. This error is due to the fact that the DDPM scheme is unable to retrieve the rank of $\bSigma$ (that has also be observed for the discrete schemes in \cite{pierret_diffusion_models_gaussian_distributions_2024}). Note that this error can be managed by modifying the noise schedule, as explored in \cite{Strasman_analysis_noise_schedule_SGM_2025_TMLR}.


