\subsection{Aerial and Space-Based Quantum Networks}
Quantum communication in non-terrestrial domains has emerged as a pivotal enabler for scalable, long-distance entanglement distribution and global QKD. Aerial and space-based quantum networks leverage unique physical and atmospheric conditions—such as low decoherence in vacuum and reduced photon scattering at high altitudes—to extend the reach of quantum protocols beyond the limitations of terrestrial fiber and classical repeaters.

\subsubsection{Satellite Constellations for Global QKD}
Satellite QKD networks enable long-distance entanglement and key exchange over thousands of kilometers, a scale impractical for fiber-based systems due to exponential signal loss. The Chinese Micius satellite demonstrated intercontinental QKD and entanglement distribution, marking a foundational milestone for space-based quantum networks~\cite{Liao2017SatelliteMicius}. Ongoing efforts, such as the European Union’s SAGA and ESA’s QKD infrastructure projects, aim to deploy constellations of low-Earth orbit (LEO) and medium-Earth orbit (MEO) satellites that support global quantum communication coverage~\cite{Sidhu2021SpaceComms, Macci2023QuantumSatellites}. These systems promise applications ranging from secure global messaging to resilient cryptographic services across continents.

\subsubsection{UAV-Enabled Entanglement Swarms}
Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) are increasingly integrated into quantum network architectures as agile, short-range nodes for QKD and entanglement relay. Their mobility, altitude flexibility, and low deployment cost make them ideal for tactical quantum communication in remote or rapidly changing environments. Thakkar et al.~\cite{Thakkar2021QuantumUAV} introduced a quantum cryptography-as-a-service framework for UAV swarms, demonstrating use cases such as encrypted telemetry and battlefield command chains. These UAVs can also function as airborne quantum repeaters, facilitating entanglement extension between stationary terrestrial nodes or mobile edge devices.

\subsubsection{High-Altitude Platform Systems (HAPS)}
High-altitude platforms such as stratospheric balloons and solar-powered aircraft offer a stable middle layer between satellites and ground stations. These systems benefit from stable atmospheric conditions, reduced turbulence, and direct line-of-sight communication with  satellites and terrestrial endpoints. As Al-Hraishawi et al.~\cite{AlHraishawi2024QuantumNTN} highlight, HAPS can act as dynamic relay stations to overcome terrestrial obstructions or weather disruptions impacting fiber-based quantum links. HAPS can maintain entanglement fidelity across nodes in quantum scenarios by minimizing optical beam divergence and compensating for polarization drift.

\subsubsection{Space-Air-Ground Integrated Quantum Networks (SAGIQN)}
Integrating satellite, aerial, and terrestrial quantum infrastructures forms the architecture of SAGIQNs—multi-tiered networks that dynamically adapt to varying fidelity, coherence, and visibility constraints. Xu et al.~\cite{Xu2022QSATUAV} proposed an integrated SAGIQN framework that merges LEO satellites, UAVs, and ground stations, enabling seamless quantum handshakes across atmospheric boundaries. These networks are designed to optimize entanglement routing and key provisioning across hybrid layers using adaptive software-defined networking (SDN) and fidelity-aware protocol stacks. They are expected to underpin the future global quantum internet by offering  redundancy and scalability in heterogeneous quantum environments.


\subsection{Quantum-Enhanced IoT and Healthcare Systems}
Healthcare and smart environments are increasingly integrated with quantum technologies to secure data-intensive applications, enhance diagnostic precision, and guarantee privacy. As digital transformation in medical systems accelerates, the confluence of Quantum Internet of Things (QIoT), QKD, and edge-based diagnostics forms a foundational layer of future medical infrastructure.

\subsubsection{Secure Quantum IoT for Smart Environments}
Quantum-enhanced IoT systems, or QIoT, leverage QKD to secure communications between sensors, edge devices, and data aggregators. This guarantees information-theoretic security even against quantum adversaries. Alipour et al.~\cite{Alipour2024QIoT} propose a layered QIoT architecture capable of managing qubit-enabled telemetry, fault tolerance, and authenticated control in healthcare and industrial IoT. Integrating QKD channels with classical networks ensures continuous trust in dynamic environments such as smart homes and hospitals.

\subsubsection{Entangled Medical Sensors and Diagnostics}
Entangled photon pairs offer new paradigms in biomedical sensing by improving sensitivity and minimizing measurement disturbance. Diagnostic applications range from low-dose quantum imaging to entangled MRI frameworks, where paired photons enhance resolution and reduce quantum noise~\cite{Rasool2023QuantumHealthcare}. Dhinakaran et al.~\cite{Dhinakaran2024QMedicalThings} highlight how such sensors embedded in wearable devices can monitor patient vitals securely and transmit encrypted data using lightweight QKD-based authentication.

\subsubsection{Privacy-Preserving Quantum Edge in Health Clouds}
The edge computing platforms that are empowered by quantum protocols can enforce privacy in federated healthcare learning and diagnosis-sharing systems. Javeed et al.~\cite{Javeed2024FederatedQIoT} shows that quantum-enhanced edge nodes can perform entropy-aware data validation while maintaining coherence between remote diagnostics centers and cloud hubs. These systems leverage PQC and QKD for fine-grained access control and anonymized patient records. Additionally, blockchain-based quantum medical ledgers ensure the immutability of diagnostic trails~\cite{Sabrina2024PostQuantumBlockchain}.



\subsection{Defense, Surveillance, and Critical Infrastructure}
Applying quantum communication to defense, surveillance, and critical infrastructure represents one of emerging quantum networks' most strategically significant domains. Governments and defense contractors are exploring quantum-secured military protocols, battlefield entanglement channels, and quantum radar systems globally to build resilient, low-latency, zero-trust frameworks that remain operational under electronic warfare or post-quantum cyberattack scenarios.

\subsubsection{Entanglement-Backed Battlefield Networks}
Entanglement-enabled battlefield communication systems aim to replace conventional symmetric-key cryptography with real-time quantum key generation via QKD or entanglement swapping. These systems ensure authentication, forward secrecy, and resistance to eavesdropping—even under active adversarial conditions. As reported by Dakić et al.~\cite{Dakic2021QuantumBattlefield}, quantum-secured command and control systems allow entangled units to share tactical information in a delay-minimized and spoof-proof manner. This paradigm is particularly valuable for mobile ad-hoc quantum networks (MAQNs), such as those used in high-mobility ground or aerial troop deployments.

\subsubsection{Quantum Radar and Surveillance Swarms}
Quantum radar systems exploit entanglement and quantum illumination to detect stealth objects and suppress classical radar noise. These systems offer higher resolution, lower signal-to-noise ratios, and enhanced jamming resistance compared to classical systems~\cite{Lloyd2008QuantumRadar, Barzanjeh2020MicrowaveRadar}. Surveillance swarms of UAVs or microsatellites embedded with quantum sensors leverage non-local correlations to form an entangled awareness mesh, boosting coordination and real-time threat detection. Integrating quantum-enhanced LiDAR and microwave quantum radar enables next-gen border security and airspace monitoring.

\subsubsection{Zero-Trust Architectures for National Quantum Defense}
Zero-trust architectures in national defense aim to eliminate assumptions about trust between subsystems, even within internal networks. Quantum technologies reinforce this by enabling tamper-evident communication paths, distributed authentication via QKD, and entropic audit trails for sensitive data transmission. According to Giannoni-Crystal~\cite{GiannoniCrystal2023CyberDefense}, entanglement-based authentication and blockchain-tracked telemetry are considered for space-borne infrastructure such as lunar satellites and missile tracking arrays. These systems utilize layered OSI redesigns with quantum-native session and transport layers that ensure continuous identity validation, data integrity, and protocol fidelity.


\subsection{Entropy-Secured Digital Ecosystems}
Quantum-enabled digital ecosystems are shifting from centralized infrastructures to entropic, identity-agnostic, and cryptographically self-sovereign environments. These next-generation systems incorporate quantum entropy sources, decentralized governance mechanisms, and information-theoretic secure protocols across multi-agent and Web3 layers.

\subsubsection{Quantum Identity and Decentralized Key Management}
The decentralized digital identity (DDID) concept leverages QKD and verifiable credentials to decouple trust from legacy certificate authorities. Xu et al.~\cite{xu2023quantumblockchain} describe a quantum-enhanced blockchain with identity tokens bound to QKD-based entanglement sessions. These systems avoid centralized identity exposure, supporting pseudonymous authentication with quantum-resilient entropy tokens. Paing et al.~\cite{paing2023counterfactual} further propose counterfactual Byzantine consensus protocols where user identity is encoded via zero-knowledge QKD signatures across federated ledgers.

\subsubsection{Blockchain-Backed QKD Ledgers and Access Control}
Quantum-secured blockchains are emerging as entropic backbones for identity, auditing, and access control. Ren et al.~\cite{ren2025web3quantum} introduce a framework where Web3 applications integrate QKD-derived entropy for transaction confirmation and credential hashing. Blockchain states are verified through quantum-secure hash digests that ensure forward secrecy, while the distributed ledger records the audit trail of key exchanges. Yang et al.~\cite{yang2022quantumblockchain} highlight that integrating quantum randomness into smart contract workflows enhances their resistance to future quantum decryption attacks.

\subsubsection{Zero-Knowledge Proofs and Privacy Layers}
Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) are being re-engineered to operate with quantum entropy inputs. These proofs support verifiable computation without revealing underlying data—an essential feature for privacy-preserving credentials and cross-domain interactions. Sharma~\cite{sharma2025quantumhashing} introduces a model of quantum collision resistance that merges hash lattice structures with probabilistic encoding, allowing ZKPs to operate on encrypted quantum metadata. Sezer and Akleylek~\cite{sezer2025lattice} present lattice-based ZKP protocols that survive NISQ (Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum) attacks, forming the backbone of IoMT (Internet of Medical Things) credential exchanges.

\subsubsection{Web3 Entropy Assurance via Quantum Entanglement}
The entropy guarantees of Web3 applications depend on randomization sources that withstand quantum-level adversarial modeling. Integrating physical quantum entropy—through photon randomness and entanglement collapse—into blockchain mining, token generation, and smart contract triggers is a key trend. Tuli et al.~\cite{tuli2024metaverseq} demonstrate a metaverse identity platform where QKD-enforced credentials are cross-validated across edge devices, satellites, and virtual machines using a probabilistic trust fabric. Michelagnoli~\cite{michelagnoli2023quantumid} outlines the W3ID protocol—an entangled identity schema designed to support quantum-resilient Web3 avatars and decentralized biometric proofs.



