\subsubsection{Quantum-as-a-Service (QaaS) Platforms}
QaaS architectures allow users to access quantum processors remotely via cloud interfaces, abstracting away hardware complexity. Leading platforms such as IBM Quantum Experience, Amazon Braket, Microsoft Azure Quantum, and Rigetti’s Quantum Cloud Services offer hardware-agnostic SDKs (e.g., Qiskit, Braket SDK, Cirq) that enable scalable quantum application development~\cite{Nguyen2024QuantumCloud}. These platforms also integrate simulation, noise modeling, and hybrid classical-quantum workflows. Kapoor~\cite{Kapoor2024CloudFuture} outlines how modern QaaS infrastructures support real-time scheduling, coherence tracking, and access-control policies tailored to entanglement-aware workloads.

\subsubsection{Federated Quantum Learning across Data Centers}
Federated quantum learning (FQL) enables distributed training of quantum machine learning models across multiple cloud nodes without exposing local datasets. This is essential in privacy-sensitive applications such as genomics, finance, and defense. Nguyen et al.~\cite{Nguyen2024QuantumCloud} propose a multi-provider architecture that connects quantum cloud backends (IonQ, Rigetti, D-Wave) using shared entanglement pools and quantum channels. This approach balances privacy, compute efficiency, and entanglement distribution by dynamically reassigning learning loads to optimal coherence zones. Cross-layer orchestration (Layers 0–7) is enhanced using software-defined quantum networks that adapt routing based on fidelity metrics.

\subsubsection{Multi-Tenant Quantum Access Management}
Unlike classical virtualization, quantum access management must address coherence conflicts, crosstalk, and entropy isolation between users. Multi-tenant quantum systems introduce scheduler-aware job isolation, dynamic circuit transpilation, and qubit remapping techniques to enforce user separation. Laouid et al.~\cite{Laouid2025QaaS} discuss fine-grained access control models for hybrid QaaS platforms that leverage zero-trust mechanisms and entropy tagging. These solutions integrate blockchain-backed job attestation and QKD-derived credentials to ensure task integrity and quantum-state accountability.
These advancements make quantum cloud infrastructure a viable and secure backbone for quantum-native applications—bridging access, intelligence, and multi-domain orchestration across the layered OSI stack.


\subsection{Quantum Networking for Smart Cities}
Quantum networking is anticipated to play a transformative role in smart city infrastructures' security, efficiency, and trustworthiness. As vehicular networks, urban surveillance, and citizen identity systems become increasingly decentralized and sensitive to data integrity, quantum-enforced communication protocols offer unique advantages in securing physical and digital urban ecosystems.

\subsubsection{Entangled Vehicular Communications (Q-V2X)}
Quantum vehicle-to-everything (Q-V2X) leverages entangled photon pairs and QKD to secure communications between vehicles, roadside infrastructure, and cloud platforms. Venkatesh et al.~\cite{venkatesh2024qv2x} propose an entanglement-assisted vehicular framework where secure QKD-based handshakes allow authenticated and real-time inter-vehicle data exchanges. Fowler et al.~\cite{fowler2023securev2x} demonstrated the deployment of FSO-QKD in V2X security, providing quantum-derived keys compliant with IEEE 1609.2 standards. Stavdas et al.~\cite{stavdas2024quantumv2i} further integrate quantum key distribution within software-defined vehicular infrastructures (SDN-V2I), achieving fidelity-aware routing and fast key refresh in urban congestion scenarios.

\subsubsection{Quantum Traffic Infrastructure and Surveillance}
Entanglement-enforced surveillance systems in smart cities support secure, interference-resilient monitoring across intersections, borders, and public transportation hubs. Bhattacharya et al.~\cite{bhattacharya2021surveillance} discuss the integration of UAV-mounted quantum sensors for detecting signal spoofing and vehicular anomalies in real time. Quantum LiDAR and radar systems, operating with edge-coherent nodes, enable low-latency coordination for emergency response. Quantum swarm intelligence, as explored by Jahid et al.~\cite{jahid2023convergence}, enhances distributed surveillance through collaborative entanglement exchange between sensors embedded in smart city poles, lampposts, and mobile agents.

\subsubsection{Secure Public Service Identity and Mobility Tokens}
Smart city governance increasingly relies on tokenized identity schemes for mobility services, digital licenses, and civic credentials. Kumar et al.~\cite{kumar2024identity} present a post-quantum IoT framework where QKD-backed tokens are used for autonomous tolling, parking access, and real-time transit authentication. Chawla and Mehra~\cite{chawla2023postquantum} propose a convergence model where blockchain and quantum certificates co-manage digital identities for citizens, integrating zero-knowledge credentials and entropic trust mechanisms into municipal applications. Such systems prevent spoofing and support cross-domain authorization across healthcare, mobility, and governance interfaces.



