Quantum sensors—leveraging superposition and tunneling effects—are being deployed for industrial anomaly detection, material analysis, and environmental sensing. Such sensors outperform classical noise immunity and temporal resolution devices, crucial for adaptive control systems~\cite{Jahid2023SmartConvergence}. Moreover, real-time coherence preservation is essential to ensure synchronization across distributed factories and between cyber-physical entities~\cite{Senapati2025Battery,Charter2024CyberPhysical}. These coherence-aligned sensor networks enable applications like predictive maintenance and nano-level assembly monitoring.




\subsubsection{Quantum-Safe Supply Chain Communication}
Quantum manufacturing also relies on secure supply chain networks. Protecting proprietary data in transit is vital from design blueprints to logistical metadata. Post-quantum and QKD-based communication frameworks ensure tamper-resilience in the global production chain~\cite{Rojek2024SupplyChain6G}. Additionally, digital twin-enhanced supply chains~\cite{Vudugula2025CarbonSupply} facilitate simulation of logistics, energy usage, and production risk using entropic verification and blockchain-anchored credentials. These innovations aim to ensure authenticity and traceability in multi-tier supply networks.

Together, these advancements enable a future where industrial automation is not only intelligent but also quantum-resilient, trust-centric, and dynamically adaptive across supply chains and robotic ecosystems.



