Quantum network simulators such as NetSquid~\cite{castro2025netsquid} and QuNetSim~\cite{9465750} are increasingly embedded into academic curricula at the graduate and undergraduate levels. These tools offer modular modeling of quantum hardware, fidelity-aware channel abstraction, and stack-layer protocol implementation. Joubert~\cite{joubert2024quisp} extends these frameworks using Julia for performance optimization and curriculum flexibility. Courses now feature simulation-based labs where students use open-source packages to implement QKD, entanglement routing, and coherence tracking.

\subsubsection{Quantum Virtual Labs and Remote Protocol Debugging}
Remote-access quantum labs provide students and researchers with sandboxed environments to test code on  emulated and real quantum devices. Platforms like IBM Quantum and AWS Braket offer integrated IDEs with live circuit transpilation and decoherence visualization. Pizzorno~\cite{pizzorno2022qkdsim} demonstrates how visual debugging tools integrated with QKD simulators help users trace fidelity drift, noise interference, and gate-layer failures. These virtual infrastructures lower the entry barrier to quantum experimentation while maintaining pedagogical rigor and experimental accuracy.



