Quantum sensing and communication technologies offer transformative potential in Earth system monitoring, ranging from seismic networks to atmospheric telemetry. Their ability to detect minute variations in physical parameters and entanglement-secured transmission supports distributed precision sensing critical for sustainable development and disaster preparedness.

\subsubsection{Quantum Sensing in Climate and Seismic Networks}
Due to their ultra-sensitive detection capabilities, Quantum sensors such as atom interferometers and optically pumped magnetometers are being integrated into climate and seismic networks. These instruments outperform classical devices in precision and noise tolerance, supporting applications in geodesy, greenhouse gas monitoring, and tectonic surveillance~\cite{ElMorsalani2024QuantumSensors, Sakoglu2020InfraredSensing}. Lv et al.~\cite{Lv2022SeismicReview} review cable-free quantum seismographs identifying low-magnitude tremors and underground activity, critical for early warning systems in vulnerable regions.

