Distributed acoustic sensing (DAS) and fiber-optic interferometry enable vast-scale environmental instrumentation. Fernández-Ruiz et al.~\cite{FernandezRuiz2022DAS} illustrate the use of deep-ocean optical fibers as passive seismic arrays with quantum-enhanced resolution, capable of capturing fine-grained shifts in tectonic plates. Moreover, M. Ashfaq et al.~\cite{Ashfaq2024IoTQuantumEnv} emphasize the role of quantum-assisted IoT in measuring weather anomalies, pollution, and soil moisture over heterogeneous terrains. The convergence of quantum networks and Earth observation systems is expected to power next-gen precision climate science.




\section{Evaluation Framework}
The performance evaluation of quantum-converged OSI stacks requires metrics that move beyond classical throughput and delay, incorporating uniquely quantum considerations such as entanglement stability, entropy throughput, coherence maintenance, and adaptive orchestration efficiency. A foundational metric is entanglement fidelity, which measures the closeness of distributed quantum states to ideal Bell pairs. This fidelity is critical in teleportation, QKD, and quantum circuit construction, and is commonly measured through tomography and NetSquid trace simulations~\cite{castro2025netsquid, bassoli2021quantumcomm}. Complementing fidelity is entropy throughput, which accounts for the quantum channel's capacity to carry usable information in the presence of noise and decoherence. Simulations using tools like QuNetSim and QuISP quantify entropy via Von Neumann entropy and mutual information gain~\cite{9465750, abane2025entanglementrouting}.

Another crucial evaluation dimension is cross-layer coherence latency, when a quantum state remains usable as it traverses various protocol layers—from physical to application. This latency reflects synchronization challenges and qubit lifetime constraints, especially relevant in mobile and satellite quantum networks. NetSquid and QuNetSim allow fine-grained tagging and temporal decay analysis to benchmark such latency~\cite{lavezzari2024netsquid}. As quantum networks increasingly adopt software-defined paradigms, orchestration latency—especially that controlled by LLMs or RL agents—emerges as a critical metric. It measures the delay between fidelity degradation detection and reconfiguration response. Such adaptability reflects the efficiency of the proposed Cognitive Intent Plane (Layer 8) in maintaining service reliability~\cite{pedone2023cyberquantum}.
These metrics converge in composite testbed evaluations involving NetSquid, QuNetSim, and DOE testbeds, where teleportation fidelity, QKD performance, entropy loss, and intelligent session repair are jointly assessed. Realistic validation includes quantum SDN routing under entanglement decay, AI agent responsiveness in orchestrating layer behavior, and entropy-fidelity trade-offs for hybrid classical-quantum control loops. This comprehensive framework ensures stack-level performance benchmarking aligned with state-of-the-art quantum networking research and deployment requirements.


\subsection{Entanglement Fidelity}
Entanglement fidelity is a foundational metric in evaluating the integrity and utility of quantum communication links. It quantifies how accurately an entangled quantum state, such as a Bell pair, is preserved during transmission, storage, and interaction with noise. Defined formally as the overlap between the experimentally distributed state and the ideal target state, fidelity provides a statistical measure of coherence maintenance. Mathematically, this is expressed as \( F = \langle \psi_{\text{ideal}} | \rho_{\text{actual}} | \psi_{\text{ideal}} \rangle \), where \( \rho \) is the density matrix of the actual distributed state. High fidelity—typically above 0.9—is essential for ensuring correctness in quantum teleportation, QKD, and gate-level entanglement swapping. Measurement of fidelity in practice is carried out through quantum state tomography or swap tests, while simulators like NetSquid and QuNetSim provide event-based approximations for larger-scale network studies~\cite{castro2025netsquid, diadamo2023qunetsim}. In protocol stacks, entanglement fidelity impacts layers from the physical substrate to the session layer, influencing  the error correction burden and the viability of circuit construction across long distances or layered relays. Thus, fidelity is not merely a physical-layer concern, but a stack-wide performance anchor in quantum OSI redesign.



\subsection{Entropy Throughput}
Entropy throughput is a quantum-native extension of classical data rate metrics, designed to measure the rate at which usable quantum information can be transmitted across a network channel, particularly under noise and decoherence conditions. Unlike classical bit-rate calculations, entropy throughput captures quantum channels' probabilistic and non-deterministic characteristics, where noise or loss can drastically alter information capacity. It is typically evaluated using the von Neumann entropy of a quantum state or the mutual information between sender and receiver, reflecting how much quantum data is preserved after transmission. This metric becomes particularly relevant when considering entangled state distribution, quantum error correction performance, and teleportation-based communication. In practical scenarios, tools like NetSquid, QuISP, and QuNetSim simulate entropy variations under fluctuating coherence or QBER (quantum bit error rate), enabling the profiling of throughput across layers 1 to 4~\cite{abane2025entanglementrouting, 9465750}. For hybrid systems, entropy throughput also governs how quantum and classical channels are jointly optimized, balancing information gain with state degradation. As such, this metric offers a realistic lens for evaluating quantum OSI stacks where redundancy, compression, and noise adaptation must be dynamically orchestrated.


\subsection{Cross-Layer Coherence Latency}
Cross-layer coherence latency is a critical metric in quantum networking, capturing the time delay introduced when quantum states—particularly qubits or entangled pairs—traverse multiple protocol stack layers while undergoing decoherence. Unlike classical packet delay, this form of latency reflects transmission time and accounts for the degradation of quantum information as it interacts with various processing modules at each layer. It is defined as the time between qubit initialization (typically at Layer 0 or 1) and its confirmed usability at higher layers such as Layer 3 or 4. This measure becomes essential in protocols requiring multi-hop teleportation, quantum memory access, or real-time routing of entangled links, where even minimal decoherence can lead to protocol failure.

Tools for evaluating cross-layer coherence latency include time-tagged simulation logs from platforms like NetSquid and QuNetSim, which enable event-based modeling of fidelity decay across layered transitions~\cite{diadamo2023qunetsim, castro2025netsquid}. For example, Lavezzari et al.~\cite{lavezzari2024netsquid} demonstrated how entanglement swapping operations incur coherence penalties when switching between intermediate nodes and stack layers, a factor critical for maintaining synchrony in mobile or satellite-based quantum networks. Timeout models and decoherence-aware scheduling policies can also be implemented to mitigate these effects, ensuring layer synchronization aligns with quantum memory coherence times. Ultimately, this metric offers a lens into the hidden time costs of quantum stack processing and supports the optimization of quantum control loops, buffer lifetimes, and inter-layer orchestration mechanisms.


\subsection{AI-Based Adaptability and Orchestration Latency}
AI-based adaptability and orchestration latency is a higher-layer quantum networking metric that quantifies the responsiveness of intelligent agents—particularly LLMs, RL agents, and software-defined controllers—in detecting network variations and reconfiguring quantum sessions accordingly. In classical networks, orchestration latency often refers to control-plane convergence. However, the challenge in quantum environments is amplified due to the fragile nature of entanglement, decoherence dynamics, and probabilistic link quality. This metric is defined as the elapsed time between detecting fidelity degradation or path failure and deploying a corrective action such as rerouting, link regeneration, or stack reconfiguration.
Measurement tools include policy logs of AI agents, decision tree execution timing, and event-driven traceability through NetSquid or NS-3+Quantum environments. Such systems capture the AI orchestration loop: signal detection, action selection, and execution latency. For instance, Pedone~\cite{pedone2023cyberquantum} outlines a cognitive network architecture where RL agents dynamically adapt session state based on real-time noise models and fidelity metrics. These agents use entropic feedback from lower layers to anticipate circuit collapse and suggest preemptive migration strategies. Further, LLM-based orchestration, as explored by Chaoub et al.~\cite{chaoub2024llm6g}, provides natural-language policy translation, allowing user intent to be interpreted and executed across the quantum stack with minimal delay.

This metric is particularly relevant for the proposed Layer 8—Cognitive Intent Plane—where stack behavior must be proactively orchestrated using AI. Low orchestration latency implies high adaptability and service continuity under volatile conditions such as mobile quantum nodes, adversarial interference, or intermittent QKD link failures. Therefore, this metric is not merely an operational delay figure but a key indicator of network intelligence and resilience.


\subsection{Composite Evaluation Scenarios}
Composite evaluation scenarios are essential to validate quantum-converged networks' functional correctness, resilience, and scalability beyond isolated metric analysis. These scenarios simulate full-stack behaviors by incorporating entanglement management, classical-quantum control feedback, and AI-driven orchestration across end-to-end communication paths. Leading platforms such as NetSquid, QuNetSim, and QuISP enable event-based modeling of teleportation, QKD, entanglement routing, and session-level control under varying fidelity conditions~\cite{castro2025netsquid, diadamo2023qunetsim, joubert2024quisp}. These simulators allow researchers to analyze protocol interactions across the OSI stack, from the physical quantum substrate to the application and cognitive orchestration layers.
In practical test scenarios, researchers often integrate entropy-fidelity trade-offs with classical Quality-of-Service (QoS) indicators such as delay jitter, energy consumption, and packet drop rate. For instance, during entanglement routing, one may examine how fidelity decay impacts session persistence, or how entropy throughput fluctuates under energy-constrained edge devices. Such hybrid analysis reveals systemic weaknesses in stack-layer synchronization and provides insights into entropy-aware scheduling and load balancing mechanisms~\cite{abane2025entanglementrouting}.

Moreover, composite evaluation frameworks are increasingly enhanced through co-simulation environments that combine quantum layers with classical middleware, SDN-Q controllers, and physical-layer emulation. These testbeds support dynamic topologies (e.g., moving UAV nodes or LEO satellites) and real-time fidelity feedback to higher control planes. As shown by Lavezzari et al.~\cite{lavezzari2024netsquid}, these models provide realistic approximations of mobile quantum networks and enable developers to evaluate coherence degradation, orchestration latency, and control-plane efficiency simultaneously. These comprehensive scenarios are the backbone for benchmarking next-generation quantum OSI architectures under real-world conditions.

