\section{Open Challenges and Future Research Directions}
Despite substantial advances in quantum networking and OSI stack redesign, several core challenges remain unresolved. These challenges stem from fundamental physics limitations, protocol incompatibility, intelligence orchestration latency, and a lack of interoperable architectural standards. This section dissects three key categories of emerging hurdles, supported by recent research across academia and industry. The path toward a scalable, secure, and programmable quantum internet necessitates multidisciplinary advancements that bridge physical-layer engineering, stackable protocol designs, quantum-classical control, and metrics development. This section identifies major research frontiers critical to realizing end-to-end quantum networking infrastructures.

\subsection{Real-Time Stackless Design Models}
Traditional layered network architectures are suboptimal for quantum systems due to the fragility of quantum states and the strict timing requirements for maintaining coherence. The concept of "stackless" networking—where layers interact non-linearly or are bypassed for real-time fidelity management—is gaining traction~\cite{granelli2022architecture}. Entanglement must be preserved even during rapid topological changes in mobile quantum systems like UAV swarms and satellite relays. Real-time stackless designs eliminate buffering delays and reduce qubit decoherence by enabling vertical optimization across OSI layers~\cite{jing2023stackless}. However, modeling such architectures is complex, requiring new abstractions that unify quantum physics with network scheduling~\cite{vista2024qmlstack}.

\subsection{Co-Evolution of LLM and QML Agents}
While LLMs have shown promise in translating user intents and policies into programmable control logic, their integration with QML agents remains nascent. Hybrid architectures, such as those proposed by Yu et al.~\cite{yu2024llmnetworking} and Choudhury et al.~\cite{choudhury2023llmqml}, suggest a dual-agent paradigm where QML agents handle entanglement forecasting, and LLMs manage cross-domain policy translation. However, synchronizing inference time with real-time fidelity feedback, managing training entropy, and ensuring cross-platform compatibility present significant hurdles. This co-evolution also demands quantum-aware datasets, as LLMs pre-trained on classical corpora may lack contextual relevance~\cite{zhu2023llmquantum}.

\subsection{Interoperability in Hybrid Classical–Quantum Space}
Perhaps the most pressing challenge is the absence of a unified interoperability standard for hybrid networks. Quantum-classical interfaces—like control-plane protocols, QKD-authenticated handshakes, and entanglement metadata layers—vary drastically across platforms~\cite{rane2024aiindustry}. Vista~\cite{vista2024qmlstack} and Emu~\cite{emu2024hybridinterop} argue that without entropic session keys, fidelity-aware API definitions, and SDN-Q controllers, seamless communication between terrestrial and satellite quantum nodes will remain limited. Moreover, evolving quantum internet initiatives (e.g., EU Quantum Flagship, ARPA-Q) have yet to reach consensus on standardized middleware for routing, error correction, and control-plane abstraction~\cite{frank2023quantumstack, ivanova2024interconnect}.
These unresolved issues underscore the need for open-source protocol libraries, stack-agnostic testbeds, and AI-assisted verification tools that can scale with next-generation quantum networks' computational and physical complexity.





\subsection{Stackable Architectures for the Quantum Internet}
Future quantum networks require modular, composable stacks that accommodate heterogeneity across devices, topologies, and applications. Such architectures must abstract fidelity-aware routing, quantum memory control, and entanglement provisioning into programmable planes~\cite{picchi2023architectures}. Aman et al.~\cite{aman2024quantumstack} propose a stack blueprint where layers are dynamically compiled based on qubit quality, coherence decay models, and node capabilities. Unlike rigid OSI analogues, this compositional model treats stack layers as fungible components orchestrated by quantum control planes. Cross-domain adaptation—e.g., terrestrial-to-space entanglement switching—also demands agile layer integration policies, such as those studied in the QuNet and EU Quantum Flagship projects~\cite{dahlberg2019link, metodi2021architecture}.

\subsection{Cross-Layer QEC}
Conventional QEC models like Shor and surface codes focus on hardware layers. However, real-world quantum internet scenarios necessitate error correction policies that span network, transport, and session layers~\cite{khan2024qec, pierucci2024qecsurvey}. On~\cite{on2024qwn} highlights how fidelity decay across relay networks can be mitigated through multi-hop QEC zones, dynamically instantiated during entanglement swapping. Research from Munro et al.~\cite{munro2021repeater} and Van Meter et al.~\cite{vanmeter2022stack} suggests interleaving classical parity metadata within QKD or teleportation streams can prevent stack reinitialization and lower session error rates. Future protocols may embed QEC triggers at middleware levels to detect and reroute around collapsing links.

\subsection{Programmable Quantum SDN Interfaces}
The extension of software-defined networking (SDN) principles to quantum networks enables dynamic control over entanglement path selection, circuit provisioning, and multi-domain coordination~\cite{pierucci2024quantumsdn, chakraborty2023sdsn}. Stack elements must expose programmable APIs that handle coherence parameters, circuit cost vectors, and qubit lifespan. Research from Dahlberg et al.~\cite{dahlberg2022netqasm} and Siddiqui et al.~\cite{siddiqui2023qsdn} shows promise in SD-Q controllers capable of dynamic reconfiguration under fidelity-aware constraints. Moreover, programmable middleware frameworks—like QIR and QCoDeS—are emerging as abstraction layers that convert logical instructions into hardware-specific gates~\cite{mosk2022qir}. Future interfaces must accommodate AI/ML agents that can orchestrate entangled services autonomously. Fig.~\ref{fig:sdqn_architecture} presents a programmable quantum network architecture featuring SDN-controlled switches and fidelity-aware routing, foundational for scalable quantum data planes.
