To encode the colours in the Latin squares $P$ and $Q$, we create  $3n^2$ colour variables $A_{i,j,r}$ for $P$ and another $3n^2$ colour variables $B_{i,j,r}$ for $Q$ where $0\leq i,j < n$ and $ r\in \{\texttt{w},\texttt{g},\texttt{d}\}$. 
A cell $(i,j)$ in each of $P$ and $Q$ is one of white \texttt{w}, light \texttt{g}, or dark \texttt{d}. 
We demonstrate the encoding for $P$, and it similarly can be done for $Q$.

\subsubsection{Encoding exactly one colour per cell:} 

Every cell $(i,j)$ of $P$ for $0\leq i,j<n$ is assigned exactly one colour $r \in \{\texttt{w},\texttt{g},\texttt{d}\}$. 
This fact is encoded with the constraint $$\bigvee_{r \in \{\texttt{w},\texttt{g},\texttt{d}\}}A_{i,j,r}$$ that ensures cell $(i,j)$ is assigned \emph{at least} one of the three colours, and the 
constraints 
\[
\bigwedge_{r,s \in \{\texttt{w},\texttt{g},\texttt{d}\}, r\neq s}\left(A_{i,j,r} \limp \neg A_{i,j,s}\right)
\]
ensure that cell $(i,j)$ is assigned \emph{at most} one colour. 

\subsubsection{Encoding cardinality:}

Each row in $P$ is further constrained by satisfying a specific number of white, dark, or light cells in its first six columns and the last four columns depending on its type (see Table~\ref{tbl:transtypes} for the seven types). Sinz~\cite{sinz2005towards} established sequential counter encoding as a standard approach in SAT solving for cardinality constraints.

Using sequential counter encoding, consider a row $i \in \{0,1,\dotsc,n-1\}$ in $P$ and without loss of generality, fix a colour in $\{\texttt{w}, \texttt{g}, \texttt{d}\}$, say \texttt{w}. Let $h$ and $t$ be integers such that $h\in\{4,6\}$ 
and $0\leq t \leq h$.
To ensure that exactly $t$ of the colour variables $A_{i,0,\texttt{w}},A_{i,1,\texttt{w}},A_{i,2,\texttt{w}}, \dots, A_{i,h-1,\texttt{w}}$ are 
assigned true, we introduce the auxiliary Boolean variable $S_{j,k}$ for $0\leq j\leq h$ and $1\leq k\leq t$ 
which is set to true if and only if there are exactly $k$ colour variables among the first $j$ literals 
being considered in 
a given row. 
Note that 
$S_{j,0}$ is true because selecting $0$ items from the first $j$ elements is always possible. 
Similarly, 
$S_{0,k}$ is false because selecting $k>0$ items from an empty set is impossible.

Thus, for each row $i$ with $0 \leq i < n$, for $h\in\{4,6\}$, for $0\leq t \leq h$, and for each colour $r \in \{\texttt{w}, \texttt{g}, \texttt{d}\}$, the cardinality constraints are:
\[
S_{j,k} \leftrightarrow (S_{j-1,k} \lor (A_{i,j-1,r} \land S_{j-1,k-1})), \text{ where } 1 \leq j \leq h, 1 \leq k \leq t.
\]
Note that we fixed a row by transversal type to generate the clauses. This is valid because each square has a clearly defined type based on the transversals it contains. 

\subsection{Symbol and Colour Correspondence}

Let variables $P_{i,j,r}$ be defined as the symbol variables of $P$ as described in Section~\ref{sec:symbol}, and the variables $A_{i,j,k}$ be defined as the colour variables of $P$ as described in Section~\ref{sec:colour}. We now describe the correspondence between these variables.

\subsubsection{Encoding colour-assignment to a cell with a given symbol:}
Consider a cell $(i,j)$ where $0 \leq i, j < n$. Then for $0 \leq r \leq 3$, the clauses
\[
    P_{i,j,r} \rightarrow A_{i,j,\texttt{w}}
    \]
ensure that if cell $(i,j)$ contains a symbol $r \in \{0,1,2,3\}$, then the cell must be white.
Also, for $4 \leq r \leq 9$, the clauses
\[
    P_{i,j,r} \rightarrow (A_{i,j,\texttt{g}} \lor A_{i,j,\texttt{d}})
    \]
ensure that if cell $(i,j)$ contains a symbol $r\in \{4,5,6,\dotsc,9\}$, then the cell must be either light or dark.

\subsubsection{Encoding symbol-assignment to a cell of a given colour:}
Consider a cell $(i,j)$ where $0 \leq i, j < n$. Then the clause
\[
    A_{i,j,\texttt{w}} \rightarrow \bigvee_{r=0}^{3} P_{i,j,r}
    \]
ensures that if cell $(i,j)$ is white, then it contains a symbol $r\in \{0,1,2,3\}$.
The clause
\[
    A_{i,j,\texttt{d}} \rightarrow \bigvee_{r=4}^{9} P_{i,j,r}
    \]
    ensures that if cell $(i,j)$ is dark, then it contains a symbol $r\in \{4,5,6,\dotsc,9\}$. The clause    
\[
    A_{i,j,\texttt{g}} \rightarrow \bigvee_{r=4}^{9} P_{i,j,r}
    \]
    ensures that if cell $(i,j)$ is light, then it contains a symbol $r$ where $r\in \{4,5,6,\dotsc,9\}$.
The above encoding is similarly done for $Q$ by using $B$ instead of $A$.

\subsubsection{Encoding that symbols in the dark cells in each column are the same:}
Since $P$ and $Q$ are both transversal representations of $L$, the colour of a cell in $L$ and the colour 
of the corresponding cell in the transversal representations $P$ and $Q$ of $L$ are the same. 
Since the colour and symbol information have a 
correspondence, a symbol on a dark cell in column $j$ of $P$ implies that column $j$ of any transversal 
involving this symbol will have a dark cell in it.

Given that $(P, Q)$ is a TRP, for $i$, $j$, $k$, $l \in\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$, the implication 
\[
A_{i,j,\texttt{d}} \land P_{i,j,k} \land Q_{l,j,k} \rightarrow B_{l,j,\texttt{d}}
\]
ensuring that if cell $(i, j)$ in $P$ is dark and contains symbol $k$, and the same symbol $k$ 
appears in cell $(l, j)$ in $Q$, then cell $(l, j)$ in $B$ must be dark. Also, the implication
\[
B_{l,j,\texttt{d}} \land P_{i,j,k} \land Q_{l,j,k} \rightarrow A_{i,j,\texttt{d}}
\]
ensuring that if cell $(l, j)$ in $Q$ is dark and contains symbol $k$, and the same symbol $k$ appears in cell $(i, j)$ in $P$, then cell $(i, j)$ in $A$ must be dark. The correctness of these constraints follows 
from Definition~\ref{def:transrep} and the correspondences discussed above.
