\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

We now discuss the results of our computational investigation into
Myrvold's results.  The computations were performed using the SAT solver
Kissat 4.0~\cite{biere2022gimsatul} run on Intel Gold 6148
Skylake processors running at 2.4~GHz and equipped with
4~GiB of memory.

Recall Myrvold showed~\cite[Thm~4.4]{myrvold1999negative},
if $P$ and $Q$ are both transversal representations of a Latin square of order ten containing
a subsquare of order four, then up to ordering there are twenty-eight possible cases
for $P$ and $Q$ and twenty of these cases can be ruled out.
The eight possible cases Myrvold left remaining are
$(\S,\X)$, $(\U,\X)$, $(\V,\X)$, $(\W,\X)$, $(\X,\X)$, $(\U,\U)$, $(\U,\W)$, and $(\W,\W)$.

