\section{Conclusion}\label{Conclusion}

In this paper we use a satisfiability (SAT) solver to investigate Myrvold's nonexistence results~\cite{myrvold1999negative}
on orthogonal triples of Latin squares of order ten.  The SAT solver automatically
rules out the cases that Myrvold ruled out, and more significantly, the SAT solver
provides explicit examples of Latin square pairs in each of the cases that
Myrvold was unable to rule out---providing an explanation for why Myrvold
was unable to rule out these cases and determining a negative resolution
to the following question left open by Myrvold:
\begin{quote}
\emph{Possibly, with a bit more ingenuity, the remaining cases can be eliminated.}
\end{quote}
We show that pairs exist in the remaining cases, and so eliminating the
remaining cases with ``a bit more ingenuity'' is probably
not achievable---at the very least,
any argument required to eliminate the remaining cases
would need to be more sophisticated in having to
rely on the existence of a third square.

In order to derive a concise and effective SAT encoding for our
search we make use of a duality between orthogonal Latin squares
and transversal representation pairs.  Although such a duality has
long been used in searches for Latin squares, we also give an explicit formulation
of how this duality arises via a composition operation on Latin squares.
We found this viewpoint useful when deriving our encoding
and surprisingly we were not able to find it expressed in prior literature.

\begin{comment}
The results presented in this paper have proven instrumental in establishing a duality between the existence of orthogonal Latin squares and mutual TRPs of Latin squares. Through Theorem~\ref{ortho-to-transv}, we have provided constructive methods and necessary conditions for transitioning between these two fundamental concepts in combinatorial design theory. This duality has facilitated the exploration and validation of numerous instances, notably exemplified by our investigation into Latin squares of order ten with a subsquare of order four.

By leveraging our encoding scheme, we verified previous findings and advanced knowledge in previously unclear territory, investigating eight out of twenty-eight potential cases exhibiting feasible instances of TRPs. Significantly, we have elucidated why these cases have not been easily dismissed through simple arguments---any argument seeking to exclude them must inherently rely on the existence of a third square, given that solutions involving only two squares as a TRP do exist. The composition operation in our encoding strongly connects to previous results even though they were established under fundamentally different assumptions.

Furthermore, we generated numerous non-isomorphic pairs within each of the eight cases. Notably, none of these pairs, despite the diversity in their construction, have been found to fit within any set of three mutually orthogonal Latin squares of order ten. This observation underscores the complexity and richness of the interplay between MOLS and TRPs, as well as the depth of their implications within combinatorial design theory.
\end{comment}

