\subsection{Adversarial Learning} 
\label{adversarial_learning}
To validate the value of Adv-BMT scenarios in downstream autonomous driving (AD) tasks, we train a reinforcement learning agent within augmented scenarios with collision interactions generated by Adv-BMT and baseline methods. To determine the quality of augmented training scenarios, we measure both driving performance and safety performance of the learned AD agent compared to learning in original training set. In our experiment, we conduct two sets of trainings. (1) open-loop training: agent is trained in the fixed training set with adversarial scenarios generated based on ground-truth ego trajectories. (2) closed-loop training: an adaptive adversarial agent attacks on current ego agent based on its recent rollout trajectory records. The adaptive adversarial agent's motion is generated by Adv-BMT and baseline. Results of both trainings are shown in table~\ref{tab:open-loop_results} and table~\ref{tab:split-results_closed_loop} respectively. 


\paragraph{Settings}
The training set contains 500 real-world scenarios randomly selected from WOMD training set. Each waymo scenario is augmented with one collision scenario, so that the ratio between waymo and safety-critical scenes is 1:1. For each method, we augments the original dataset into a new training set. For the adaptive adversarial learning experiment, we implement an adaptive generator for Adv-BMT and CAT~\cite{zhang2023catclosedloopadversarialtraining} (discard other methods due to lower inference time). We train a Twin Delayed DDPG (TD3) agent for 1 million steps using 8 random seeds to ensure robustness. We conduct the training in the MetaDrive simulator and use the TD3 algorithm for reinforcement learning (hyper-parameters listed in Appendix). We measure the average reward, average cost, average route completion rate (Compl.), and average episode cost (cost sum) for driving performance measure. We further conducted ablation studies with results shown in Appendix.



