%Since Polyak's pioneering work, heavy ball momentum has been extensively studied in minimization, yet its role in min-max games remains poorly understood. As a key component of practical min-max algorithms like Adam, this gap limit their effectiveness. In this paper, we introduce continuous-time models for heavy ball momentum with different update schemes in min-max games and analyze their dynamics. For the local aspect, we prove \textit{smaller} momentum enhances algorithmic stability by allowing local convergence over a broader range of step sizes, and alternating updates generally converge faster than simultaneous ones. For the global aspect, we find \textit{smaller} momentum guides algorithms trajectories to explore shallower slope regions of the loss landscapes, with alternating updates amplifying this effect. Surprisingly, all these phenomena differ from those observed in minimization, where \textit{larger} momentum yields similar effects. Our findings reveal fundamental differences between heavy ball momentum in min-max and minimization, advancing the theoretical understanding of optimization dynamics in adversarial learning scenarios. We also provide numerical experiments to validate our theoretical results.

Since Polyak's pioneering work, heavy ball (HB) momentum has been widely studied in minimization. However, its role in min-max games remains largely unexplored. As a key component of practical min-max algorithms like Adam, this gap limits their effectiveness. In this paper, we present a continuous-time analysis for HB with simultaneous and alternating update schemes in min-max games. Locally, we prove \textit{smaller} momentum enhances algorithmic stability by enabling local convergence across a wider range of step sizes, with alternating updates generally converging faster. Globally, we study the implicit regularization of HB, and find \textit{smaller} momentum guides algorithms trajectories towards shallower slope regions of the loss landscapes, with alternating updates amplifying this effect. Surprisingly, all these phenomena differ from those observed in minimization, where \textit{larger} momentum yields similar effects. Our results reveal fundamental differences between HB in min-max games and minimization, and numerical experiments further validate our theoretical results.






