\begin{abstract}
We analyse the migration of 300,000 academic users from Twitter/X to Bluesky between 2023 and early 2025, combining rich bibliometric data, longitudinal social-media activity, and a novel cross-platform identity-matching pipeline. We show that 18\% of scholars in our sample transitioned, with transition rates varying sharply by discipline, political expression, and Twitter engagement but not by traditional academic metrics. Using time-varying Cox models and a matched-pairs design, we isolate genuine peer influence from homophily. We uncover a striking asymmetry whereby information sources drive migration far more powerfully than audience, with this influence decaying exponentially within a week. We further develop an ego-level contagion classifier, revealing that simple contagion drives two-thirds of all exits, shock-driven bursts account for 16\%, and complex contagion plays a marginal role. Finally, we show that scholars who rebuild a higher fraction of their former Twitter networks on Bluesky remain significantly more active and engaged. Our findings provide new insights onto theories of network externalities, directional influence, and platform migration, highlighting information sources' central role in overcoming switching costs.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A cornerstone of the social media landscape is that network effects create high switching costs, making it difficult for users to exit to other platforms once entrenched. Seminal work by \citet{katzNetworkExternalitiesCompetition1985} explains how markets with strong network externalities often tend toward ``winner-take-all'' outcomes, as the value of a platform grows with each additional user. These network effects help incumbents maintain dominance \cite{galHiddenCostsFree2016,farrellChapter31Coordination2007}, especially when leaving a platform means severing ties to an established social network, which induces negative externalities even on non-users \cite{bursztynWhenProductMarkets2023}. A service like Twitter/X remains valuable to each user until a critical mass moves elsewhere, making coordinated departure inherently difficult. Even if users are dissatisfied with a platform’s governance, features, or content policies, they may remain out of fear that migrating would cost them both reach and relationships. 

Empirically, opportunities to study large-scale user departures have been rare because most dominant platforms (e.g., Facebook, Twitter) enjoyed relatively stable user bases for the past decade. Although TikTok’s rapid rise shows that new platforms can enter the market, according to TikTok itself, around 80\% of users continue to other platforms such as Instagram and Facebook, a phenomenon called multi-homing \cite{JudgementBytedanceLtd2024}. As such, fully observing a ``mass exodus'' or a tipping point event is uncommon. Consequently, there has been only limited research quantifying the forces behind collective platform departures, particularly in large, well-connected communities. \citet{cavaDriversSocialInfluence2023} model the (attempted) Twitter-to-Mastodon exodus as a contagion-like collective action: migrations clustered in communities with shared identity and high signaled commitment to migrating, underscoring how strong social influence and coordination are required to overcome network lock-in. Even in this attempted Twitter-to-Mastodon migration,  users did not scatter evenly: most flocked to a handful of popular servers and often followed their Twitter contacts there, indicating that pre-existing networks and social bonds strongly shaped the migration’s path \cite{heFlockingMastodonTracking2023}. We reaffirm these findings of clustered migrations and go beyond this previous work in multiple ways: We focus on a transition that was more successful\footnote{As of May 27, Mastodon had approximately 9.71 million users, compared to 36.17 million registered users on Bluesky.} which means that it will likely not just capture early adoptions but allow to make statements about the median user. We leverage the pre-existing data on the initial platform, which mitigates the selection bias in studying a population of users that expressed the intention to migrate \cite{cavaDriversSocialInfluence2023,heFlockingMastodonTracking2023}.

A handful of empirical case studies have demonstrated how external shocks, such as unpopular moderation decisions or controversial policy changes, can prompt significant intentions to switch platforms, often with more muted actual migrations. In the case of the 2015 Reddit controversy, alternative sites do not have the critical mass to sustain the long tail of niche content of the initial platform \cite{newellUserMigrationOnline2016}. Examining more closely the turmoil at Twitter, work based on the early period of transition behavior found some users leaving for Mastodon, Bluesky, or Threads, yet often returning to Twitter either fully or by multi-homing, highlighting the inertia produced by a large incumbent network \cite{jeongDescriptorTemporalMultinetwork2024}. Especially user groups such as journalists did not fully abandon Twitter due to its unique audience and professional utility \cite{ngJournalistsExodusNavigating2025}. 

These episodes underline how it can be difficult for alternative services to attract and then keep a critical mass of users, unless powerful push–pull factors \cite{fieslerMovingLandsOnline2020} are strong enough to overcome the threshold \cite{granovetterThresholdModelsCollective1978}.  In theoretical terms, this threshold or tipping point captures the proportion of individuals in a population who must adopt a behavior before it becomes self-sustaining or spreads widely. Depending on its distributions, a small wave of early movers can cascade into a runaway effect if enough users have sufficiently low thresholds for switching \cite{granovetterThresholdModelsCollective1978,oliverTheoryCriticalMass1985}. For example, a small core of motivated individuals can spark widespread adoption of communication technology \cite{markusCriticalMassTheory1987} or social norms \cite{centolaExperimentalEvidenceTipping2018}. 

While network effects operate globally, they also manifest in localized clusters—so-called “local network effects.”  \citet{kimLocalNetworkEffects2022} shows that the number of existing users in one’s immediate region (e.g., county) strongly predicts platform adoption, whereas users in neighboring regions have negligible influence. This aligns with our focus on first-degree networks or small sub-communities: in platform migrations, local clusters often move together. Understanding heterogeneity in adoption is thus critical \cite{steinerPlatformAdoptionSystem2016}. Certain user groups, e.g., “early adopters,” particular ideological communities, or high-prestige institutional members, may move more readily, triggering follow-on effects for more risk-averse users.

The academic community offers a uniquely high-value window into these dynamics. Academics often rely on social media to share papers, cultivate professional networks, and discuss public-facing issues \cite{jungerDoesReallyNo2020}. It has been shown that such engagement can boost citation counts \cite{chanTwitterCitations2023}. \citet{holmbergArticlesOpenAccess2020} further demonstrates that academics' platform choices are increasingly influenced by research visibility metrics, creating stronger incentives for coordinated migration when perceived citation or attention benefits emerge on alternative platforms. Past work has found that academic subgroups differ by gender, field, institutional rank, or political views in how they use Twitter \cite{gargPoliticalExpressionAcademics2025}, hinting that transitions to new platforms might also vary systematically across these dimensions. Indeed, one analysis of social scientists on Twitter found that their activity plummeted after Musk’s takeover – especially among highly visible, verified academics, whose tweeting dropped markedly as part of this quiet exodus \cite{bisbeeVibesAreDid2025}. However, \citet{wangFailedMigrationAcademic2024} found that despite many academics joining discipline-themed servers on Mastodon and forging new connections, most eventually slipped back to Twitter, citing Mastodon’s decentralization (and newer lures like Threads/Bluesky) as hurdles to sustaining engagement. 

As a result, this current moment, where changes in Twitter’s leadership and policies have led some academics to explore Bluesky, is a rare natural experiment for studying large-scale migration from a long-stable platform. Building on the literature that ties user departures to push–pull factors \cite{fieslerMovingLandsOnline2020}, network inertia \cite{farrellChapter31Coordination2007}, and tipping point theory \cite{granovetterThresholdModelsCollective1978,centolaExperimentalEvidenceTipping2018}, we examine how academic users negotiate these costs and benefits. Specifically, we speak to the dearth of empirical evidence on mass migrations in major social networks (as alluded to in \cite{delreTargetingTimingPromotional2007,golderGrowingGrowingGone2006}) by leveraging unique data capturing scholars’ transitions. We also contribute to the literature on cross-platform identity matching (e.g., \cite{shuFakeNewsDetection2017}) and the evolution of social conventions in networked settings (see \cite{mullerEffectSocialNetworks2019}). Finally, by analyzing discipline-specific and demographic-specific differences in transitioning, we contribute to the conversation about heterogeneous adoption rates \cite{markusCriticalMassTheory1987,steinerPlatformAdoptionSystem2016}. External shocks, such as policy controversies and perceived ideological shifts—can spark surprising cascades, underscoring that a single “takeoff” phase \cite{delreTargetingTimingPromotional2007,golderGrowingGrowingGone2006} is not the only route to success or failure when volatile governance decisions can intervene at any time.

Despite this rich theoretical foundation, fundamental questions remain unanswered about platform migrations. What factors determine who transitions and when? How exactly do network effects operate—do users respond to their followers' expectations or their information sources' behavior? What specific mechanisms drive contagion through networks? And do the same network effects that drive departure also sustain engagement on destination platforms? This study addresses these questions by analyzing the migration of 300,000 academics from Twitter to Bluesky. We proceed by first documenting substantial heterogeneity in transition patterns across academic disciplines and demographics, then examining how network effects drive these transitions, identifying the specific mechanisms of social influence, and finally demonstrating how network preservation affects sustained engagement on the destination platform. Our findings reveal that network effects are far from uniform—influence flows asymmetrically from information sources rather than audiences, operates primarily through simple contagion mechanisms, and proves crucial for sustained platform engagement.

We begin by showing that migration propensities vary sharply across scholars—not by their traditional academic accomplishments but by where they sit in the Twitter social graph, how politically expressive they are, and how active they are on Twitter. Specifically, we contrast variables measuring Twitter usage with publication metrics, and demonstrate that platform-centric indicators explain far more of the observed variance.

Next, we demonstrate that departures are highly synchronized within the network: scholars are much more likely to leave shortly after their direct contacts do. \citet{aral2017exercise} show that such clustering can arise from induced homophily and lead to overestimation of network effects. To guard against this, for each departing user we randomly select one of their direct contacts and one non-contact who is matched to the focal user on the same level of profile similarity as that contact. We then compare the departure likelihood of the connected peer with that of the matched non-peer. These placebo comparisons confirm that the observed timing alignment reflects genuine peer influence rather than underlying homophily.

Having established real social influence, we then ask whose behaviour matters more: the ego's audience or it's information sources. Across classic diffusion theory and platform‐migration economics, the baseline assumption is that social ties transmit influence symmetrically: thresholds count any adopted neighbour equally \cite{granovetter1978threshold,watts2002simple}, reinforcement experiments wire ties as undirected \cite{centola2007complex}, and network–externality models embed value in aggregate participation rather than directional links \cite{hill2006network,biglaiser2020value}. Empirical work increasingly questions this symmetry. Field studies show that who we listen to drives adoption more than who listens to us, e.g.\ directed advice ties outperform friendship ties in explaining consumer take-up \cite{hinz2014new}.  Yet even the most relevant migration analyses stop short of quantifying audience–versus–source effects: \citet{cava2023drivers} treat Twitter‐to‐Mastodon exposure as flowing solely from the information source and fit contagion at the community level, leaving the reverse path unmodelled. To our knowledge, no large-scale study has simultaneously estimated the opposing hazards from migrated followers (audience members) and migrated followees (information sources).  We provide the first direct evidence that these hazards are highly asymmetric. Exposure to migrated followees increases departure risk by an order of magnitude relative to exposure to migrated followers, thereby quantifying and characterising the dominance of information-source influence over audience pressure in real-world platform exit.

To move beyond aggregate hazard ratios we label every transition event with its most plausible contagion mechanism. We unify the egocentric network classification approach of \citet{andres2025}, which distinguishes contagion mechanisms from local network observations, with the endogenous–vs–exogenous shock decomposition framework of \citet{sornette2004}. We validate this unified approach using the local exposure sequence methodology from \citet{cencettiDistinguishingSimpleComplex2023}, which classifies contagion processes based on the temporal ordering of network node infections and their correlation with local network topology. Concretely, we simulate mixed-mechanism cascades on the full directed Twitter graph to train an XGBoost node–level classifier, augment the feature space with a power-law shock‐intensity term that peaks on empirically identified burst days, and apply the trained model to the observed migration data, validating accuracy on hand-labelled hold-outs (Accuracy = $0.81$, see Appendix \ref{app:classifier_perf}).  
The resulting taxonomy (Figure~\ref{fig:transition_mechanism}) shows that 66.7\% of scholar exits conform to simple contagion: individual migrated followees are enough to tip the next user. Complex contagion is rare overall and appears chiefly after Bluesky became publicly open, consistent with reinforcement becoming relevant only once a critical mass of peers is already present.  Matching the classifier outputs to political-expression quartiles (Table~\ref{tab:political_activity_by_mechanism}) reveals that politically disengaged scholars are \(83\%\) more likely to be mobilised only when an external shock lowers attention costs; engaged scholars, by contrast, follow simple contagion.

The same directional forces that propel scholars away from Twitter also bind them to Bluesky once they arrive.  We operationalise \textit{network preservation} as the share of a user’s former Twitter–academic alters that they succeed in re‐following on Bluesky. Regressing four complementary engagement outcomes on this overlap ratio, while saturating the model with calendar-month dummies and both platforms’ follower counts, yields large \& positive effects (Table~\ref{tabregression_results}). The very social capital that lowers the barrier to exit also raises the cost of subsequent abandonment: scholars who rebuild more of their informational neighbourhood remain more committed to the destination platform.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Heterogeneity in Transition Rates}
In total, 18\% of academics in our dataset transitioned from Twitter to Bluesky during the observation period. However, this masks significant heterogeneity among the different groups of users. Focussing, for example, on users who have interacted with Twitter in any way since 2024 (2022), 27.15\% (19.47\%) transitioned. 

