Figure \ref{fig:polexpr} presents Linear Probability Model (LPM) coefficient estimates illustrating the association between expressing opinions on various political topics and the probability of transitioning.  It compares marginal coefficients (circles), reflecting the effect of each topic considered independently, with joint model coefficients (triangles), which estimate the effect of each topic while accounting for expressions across all other topics. Across the marginal models, expressing an opinion on nearly every topic is positively associated with transition probability. The strongest associations occur for tweets concerning abortion rights, Twitter governance, and economic inequality. In the joint specification, these three topics remain statistically and substantively significant, indicating that they capture information beyond a general propensity to discuss politics online.

Taken together, the results show that publicly articulated positions, especially on abortion rights, economic inequality, and the governance of Twitter itself, are systematically related to early adoption of Bluesky. While the analyses are descriptive and do not establish causality, political expression constitutes an additional, pre-treatment dimension of heterogeneity that helps account for observed variation in migration rates.

\subsection{Network Effects}
Having documented substantial heterogeneity in who transitions to Bluesky, we now examine whether this variation reflects network-driven processes or purely individual characteristics. If transitions were driven solely by individual preferences, such as political orientation, academic standing, or platform dissatisfaction, we would expect them to be distributed randomly over time and across social networks. However, if network effects drive migration, we should observe two key patterns: temporal clustering of transitions around external events, and earlier transitions among users whose social contacts have already migrated.

\begin{figure}[!ht]
    \centering
    \caption{Temporal clustering of departures and placebo‐matched test of peer influence}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth, trim={50pt 0pt 50pt 20pt}, clip]{Graphics/TransitionChanges.pdf}
    \caption*{\small \textbf{a} Letter-value plots show the percentage of connections that transitioned earlier than the focal user, by quarter, for direct followers (blue), users the focal user follows (orange), and second-degree connections (green). The dashed line shows cumulative network-wide transitions. White dots indicate medians. \textbf{b} Log-odds effects from logistic regression models predicting transition in the following week for four similarity measures, comparing similarity effects (circles) versus direct connection effects (squares). Error bars represent 95\% confidence intervals.}
\label{fig:transition-changes}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:transition-changes}(\textbf{a}) reveals a consistent hierarchy: direct connections (followers and followees) transition earlier than second-degree connections, which in turn transition earlier than the network average. This pattern demonstrates clustered transitions within social neighborhoods, suggesting network effects are driving the diffusion process. However, this clustering could arise through two distinct pathways: genuine social contagion through network ties, or homophily bias where similar individuals both connect to each other and independently adopt similar behaviors. 

The log-odds effects in panel Figure \ref{fig:transition-changes}(\textbf{b}) are derived from a matching design that isolates network influence from similarity-driven selection effects. For each transitioning user, we construct matched pairs by randomly sampling one follower and identifying a non-follower with similar characteristics using an Annoy index on user embeddings (matching tolerance $\leq 0.01$). We then estimate logistic regression models predicting transition in the following week, where similarity is the z-standardized cosine similarity across four embedding spaces (Academic, Twitter Absolute, Twitter Relative, Total) and connection status is binary (1 = follower, 0 = matched non-follower). To ensure that the similarity metrics we balance on are behaviourally meaningful, we first validated all four embedding spaces: in a 1M-pair random test and a 10K-pair per-distance test, Twitter-based cosine similarity predicted closer (or, at 2–3 hops, deliberately farther) transition timing (Table \ref{tab:assort-results}).
Guided by that pattern, our placebo design fixes similarity ($\Delta \text{cos} \leq 0.01$) and contrasts a direct tie (1-hop follower) with an equally similar but non-connected alter (>1 hop). Any remaining difference in next-week transition risk therefore isolates genuine peer influence from underlying homophily. This addresses homophily bias by comparing users who are equally similar to the focal user but differ only in connection status. The similarity coefficient ($\beta_1$) captures selection effects (i.e. users transitioning because they resemble others who transitioned). The connection coefficient ($\beta_2$) represents pure network influence---the additional effect of direct connection above and beyond similarity. By holding similarity constant across connected and non-connected pairs, we eliminate confounding due to homophilous selection into social ties, ensuring that observed network effects reflect genuine social influence rather than spurious correlation.

These results establish that network effects genuinely drive transitions rather than mere similarity-based clustering. However, this raises the question about the direction of social influence: do users respond primarily to pressure from their audience (followers) or to cues from their information sources (followees)? We employ a time-varying Cox proportional hazards models to examine how the transition behavior of different types of network contacts influences individual migration decisions.

