\subsection{Contagion mechanisms}
\label{methods:contagion}
Having established that transitions exhibit substantial heterogeneity, are driven by genuine network effects rather than homophily, and flow from information sources to users, we now investigate the underlying mechanisms. The Cox models reveal the aggregate effects of network exposure, but different users may respond through distinct contagion processes. We therefore develop a classification framework to identify which specific mechanism drives each individual's transition.

Diffusion research typically distinguishes three micro-level mechanisms. Simple contagion follows classic epidemiological logic: each infected neighbour independently increases the chance of adoption \cite{granovetterThresholdModelsCollective1978}. Complex contagion requires social reinforcement: multiple exposures or a critical local fraction before adoption occurs \cite{cencettiDistinguishingSimpleComplex2023}. Spontaneous adoption captures idiosyncratic moves unrelated to peer behaviour. We initially applied the network-level classification approach of \citet{cencettiDistinguishingSimpleComplex2023} to our Twitter-to-Bluesky migration data, which suggested simple contagion governs the overall cascade, but this aggregate analysis cannot disaggregate mechanisms at the individual level.

We posit that multiple contagion mechanisms co-exist within the same network, with different users responding to different social influence processes. Therefore, we develop a node-level classification framework to identify the specific mechanism driving each individual's platform transition. \citet{andres2025} operationalises these three mechanisms at the \emph{individual} level and is directly estimable from longitudinal network data. As our data show five abrupt surges (Table \ref{tab:migration-events}) tied to platform outages, policy announcements, and the~2024 U.S. election, we adapt the framework introduced by \citet{andres2025} who assume that all variation is network–driven by adding exogenous shocks. We follow \citet{sornette2004} who model "book-sales shocks'' and find that post-peak decay follows a power-law. We incorporate the same idea by adding a global shock intensity. Table~\ref{tab:contagion_rules} formalises the four mechanisms used in our analysis. We executed \(100\) independent realisations of the mixed–contagion model described above on the entire directed Twitter follower network. Every transition event \((u,t_u)\) is converted into a single row of a design matrix \(\mathbf{X}\). We train an XGBoost classifier. Table \ref{tab:classification_results} in the appendix shows the full classification report of both classifiers. The XGBoost classifier achieves a Macro-F1 score of 0.81 at the node level. Feature importance analysis reveals distinct patterns across contagion mechanisms. Simple contagion is primarily driven by active influences (number of transitioned followees) and exposure duration. Complex contagion shows clear dependence on peer saturation (i.e., the fraction of one's network that has already transitioned) confirming that users require a higher proportion of their connections to move before adopting. Spontaneous adoption shows relatively uniform feature importance, while shock-driven transitions are dominated by shock intensity and recency. The full analysis is included in Figure \ref{fig:GAIN-LIME} (see Methods).

