Applying this classification framework to our empirical data reveals distinct temporal patterns in how different contagion mechanisms drive platform transitions (Figure \ref{fig:transition_mechanism}). Simple contagion emerges as the dominant mechanism, accounting for two-thirds of all academic transitions to Bluesky. However, the relative importance of mechanisms varies dramatically over time. In the earliest stages of platform adoption, spontaneous transitions represent a disproportionately high share as first movers cannot be influenced by network effects when few connections have yet transitioned. During baseline periods, simple contagion consistently drives the majority of transitions, while shock events create temporary surges where shock-driven adoption can account for up to 60\% of daily transitions. The five major shock periods, particularly the November 2024 US presidential election, demonstrate how exogenous events can fundamentally alter the mechanism mix, even as the absolute number of simple contagion transitions continues in the background. Complex contagion represents the smallest share (4.5\%) of transitions overall, but notably increases in the second half of our observation period, particularly after Bluesky's public release in February 2024, suggesting that network density effects become more important as the platform matures. Spontaneous adoption accounts for a notable 12.6\% of transitions and remains relatively stable over time, but virtually disappears during shock events.


