\subsection{Network Effects on Bluesky}

Our analysis thus far has focused on the factors that drive academics to leave Twitter for Bluesky. We've established that network effects powerfully shape who transitions and when, with influence flowing primarily from information sources to users through mechanisms dominated by simple contagion.

However, successful platform migration involves more than just departure: it also requires sustained engagement with the destination platform. If network effects drive transitions but do not predict engagement, this would suggest different mechanisms operate pre- and post-migration. Alternatively, if network preservation is crucial for sustained engagement, successful migrations may require coordinated reconstruction of social connections.

We therefore examine whether academics who successfully preserve their Twitter networks on Bluesky exhibit higher levels of sustained engagement on the new platform.

