Figure \ref{fig:GAIN-LIME} in the appendix presents a detailed analysis of feature importance using both XGBoost gain values and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) coefficients, revealing distinct patterns for each transition mechanism. For simple contagion, both methods identify active influences (number of transitioned followees) and exposure duration as the most important predictors, with LIME additionally highlighting network degree while showing negative coefficients for peer saturation. Complex contagion exhibits a clear focus on peer saturation (the fraction of infected followees) as the dominant feature in both gain and LIME analyses, with a strong negative association with network degree in the LIME results, confirming that complex contagion requires a higher proportion of one's network to transition before adoption becomes likely. Spontaneous adoption shows relatively flat importance across features, with shock recency being the highest LIME predictor—logically, the longer since a shock occurred, the higher the probability of spontaneous transition. For shock-driven transitions, shock intensity emerges as the only positive LIME coefficient, while shock recency shows the strongest negative association, with both features ranking highest in the gain analysis. 

\subsection{Homophily Estimation} \label{sec:methods-assorativity-estim}
To isolate network effects from homophily-driven selection effects, we construct four embedding spaces capturing different dimensions of user similarity. Academic embeddings encode bibliometric data (citations, works, impact factor) and demographics (field, gender) using standardized numeric features and one-hot encoded categorical variables. Twitter Absolute embeddings capture current platform engagement (followers, following, posts, favorites). Twitter Relative embeddings measure temporal changes in these same metrics between 2022 and 2025. Total embeddings concatenate all three spaces. All embeddings are L2-normalized and compared using cosine similarity.


We correlated cosine similarity in each embedding with absolute differences in transition timing, using two complementary designs:  
(i) a \emph{random–pair} test on 1,000,000 uniformly drawn academic pairs, and   (ii) a \emph{distance-stratified} test on 10 000 disjoint pairs at every fixed geodesic distance (1–4 hops) within the Twitter follower network. Table \ref{tab:assort-results} reports Spearman’s \(\rho\).  
