\caption*{\small Random sample \(n=1{,}000{,}000\) pairs; distance-stratified samples \(n=10{,}000\) pairs per hop. Two-tailed significance: \(^*\!p<.05\); \(^**\!p<.01\); \(^***\!p<.001\). 
}
\label{tab:assort-results}
\caption*{\small Spearman correlations between cosine similarity in four embedding spaces and absolute differences in transition timing. Negative values indicate synchronization (similar users transition closer in time); positive values indicate anti-synchronization. Academic embeddings capture bibliometric data and demographics; Twitter Absolute captures current platform engagement; Twitter Relative measures temporal changes (2022-2025); Total concatenates all spaces.}
\end{table}

The pattern mirrors the main study: (i) Twitter-based embeddings exhibit consistent negative correlations in random pairs, validating their behavioural content; (ii) academic similarity matters only for direct or near-direct ties; and (iii) for 2–3-hop pairs, Twitter-space similarity predicts \emph{longer} timing gaps. This anti-synchronization likely reflects unobserved factors (e.g., ideological differences, rival affiliations, methodological differences) that both prevent connection formation and drive divergent migration timing. These results show that our matching approach, holding similarity constant while contrasting connected versus non-connected pairs, appropriately controls for these complex, distance-dependent selection effects.

\subsection{Political Expression Analysis}
For each of the 14 political, economic, and social topics, we built hand-curated keyword dictionaries that capture the diverse terminology academics use on Twitter and Bluesky. A user is flagged as having “political expression” on a given topic if at least one of their posts contains a dictionary term; we do not attempt to distinguish stance (pro/anti/neutral) in the main analysis. We then aggregate these binary indicators to the author level and examine how simply mentioning each topic—regardless of sentiment—predicts the probability of migrating to Bluesky. The full keyword dictionary is in table \ref{sec:prompt_engineering} in the appendix.

