Our prompt engineering process evolved through several iterations. We initially started by simply asking the model which side of an issue a user stands on. This approach proved ineffective as the concepts of "pro" and "anti" were ill-defined, leading to confusion where the model struggled to categorize posts appropriately. Our next improvement came from using a standardized template where we provided the model with clear guidelines on the directionality of the prompt. This version defined a topic, provided definitions for each category, and then appended the post to the prompt. While this significantly improved the recall of the two most important categories—Pro and Anti—we remained unconvinced that the accuracy was sufficient to warrant interpretation. We then shifted our focus to reducing false positives to maximize precision rather than overall accuracy. This led to more restrictive definitions of each stance. For example, the initial definition of "pro" for economic growth changed from:

\begin{quote}
"The poster supports economic expansion, business investment, innovation, or policies that drive GDP growth."
\end{quote}

to the more specific:

\begin{quote}
"The poster explicitly endorses rapid economic growth by supporting policies or practices that directly aim to increase GDP, such as economic policies focused on GDP expansion, business growth strategies with a GDP focus, or pro-GDP growth initiatives. Excludes mentions of technology or innovation unless directly tied to GDP increase."
\end{quote}

Subsequently, we realized that the model was still frequently confused about the directionality of the prompts. While we clearly defined what each category represented, this seemed to add further confusion. We therefore altered the prompt structure to explicitly return bespoke categories for each topic. For example, for the topic of free speech, we changed to:

\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=gray!5!white,colframe=gray!75!black,title=Revised Prompt Structure]
\begin{verbatim}
"supports_free_speech": "pro",
"supports_content_restrictions": "anti",
"no_clear_stance": "neutral",
"not_about_speech": "unrelated"
\end{verbatim}
\end{tcolorbox}

During our experimentation, we labeled over 1,000 posts and employed automated solutions where a more advanced model was tasked with generating better definitions to prevent false positives. We also experimented with using larger models (i.e., GPT-4o instead of GPT-4o mini) for the labeling, and having the model provide a chain-of-thought trace for its evaluation. While these approaches led to some improvements, the prompts remained sensitive to slight changes in the posts, failed to detect obvious sarcasm, and often lacked crucial context. We therefore restrict our analysis to examining users' propensity to engage in political speech, regardless of their stance.

