%This paper introduces the Sobel kernel angle difference (SKAD) method as an alternative to Jacobian maps to characterize brain volumetric changes for dementia diagnosis. Our study reveals that SKAD provides diagnostic results comparable to Jacobian maps while being significantly more computationally- and memory-efficient. This innovation opens avenues for further exploration and refinement of diagnostic methods, potentially improving the efficiency and accessibility of dementia diagnosis and intervention. Given its success with two diverse datasets, SKAD is likely to perform well on other dementia datasets, such as NACC and HCP. While SKAD has been primarily applied to Alzheimer's Disease (AD), the underlying principles of brain atrophy and structural changes are common to various neurodegenerative diseases. Therefore, SKAD can potentially be extended to analyze other conditions, such as Parkinson’s disease, provided that the imaging data captures relevant structural alterations. Although primarily applied to AD, SKAD's principles can extend to other neurodegenerative diseases like Parkinson's, given relevant imaging data. Future research endeavors will focus on improving the sensitivity of SKAD by developing adaptive Sobel kernels that can adjust their size and orientation based on local image characteristics to capture more detailed gradient information.  Moreover, we want to augment the methodologies by incorporating explanatory mechanisms post-segmentation and brain region labeling, aiming to render the results more interpretable and clinically actionable.

This paper presents the Sobel Kernel Angle Difference (SKAD) method as a computationally-efficient alternative to Jacobian maps for characterizing brain volumetric changes in dementia diagnosis. Our findings demonstrate that SKAD delivers diagnostic results comparable to Jacobian maps while significantly reducing computational and resource demands. This advancement holds promise for enhancing the efficiency and accessibility of dementia diagnostics.

Tested on two diverse datasets, SKAD shows potential for broader applicability to other datasets, such as NACC and HCP. While primarily applied to Alzheimer’s disease and dementia, the method’s focus on brain atrophy and structural changes suggests it can be extended to other neurodegenerative diseases, such as Parkinson’s, provided relevant imaging data is available.

Future research will aim to enhance SKAD’s sensitivity by developing adaptive Sobel kernels capable of adjusting size and orientation based on local image characteristics, enabling finer gradient detection. Additionally, we plan to incorporate post-segmentation explanatory mechanisms and brain region labeling to make the results more interpretable and clinically actionable.

