%In this paper, a novel federated digital twin (DT) construction framework assisted by wireless sensing in the edge-cloud collaboration is introduced. In this framework, the DT to be built at the central cloud can be viewed as a global one splitting into and aggregating from multiple functional components, i.e., partial-DTs, created at multiple edge servers (ESs) via data collected by wireless sensors. Unlike existing work, we explore DT construction in a federated architecture where DT is dynamically evolving with heterogeneous partial-DTs. To maximize the constructed DT's model quality while minimizing all induced costs in long runs, we formulate an online optimization problem that jointly optimizes partial-DT assignments, ES-sensor associations and resource allocations. To avoid the complexity in original problem, we transform it into a two-layer hierarchical game. Particularly, in each time frame, the upper layer employs a two-sided matching game for optimal partial-DT assignments, while the lower layer employs an overlapping coalition formation game (OCFG) for optimal ES-sensor associations and resource allocations. Based on equilibrium analyses, we apply the Gale-Shapley (GS) algorithm and particularly develop an switch rules-based overlapping coalition formation (SOCF) alogrithm to find the short-term equilibria of upper-layer and lower-layer subgames, and then propose a deep reinforcement learning (DRL)-based solution to extend them into the long-term equilibrium of the hierarchical game, or equivalently the long-term optimal solution to the original problem. Simulations evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed federated DT construction framework and show the superiority of the proposed solution approach over counterparts.
