%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Concluding Remarks}
\label{sec:4}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The results on covering numbers presented in Section~\ref{sec:2} extend to other settings of which we mention three:
\smallskip \begin{itemize}
  \item locally finite sets in hyperbolic space;
  \item finite sets on the sphere;
  \item weighted points in Euclicean space.
\end{itemize} \smallskip
A claim about global covering analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:covering_number} holds in all three settings, while one about local covering analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:local_covering} holds only for the first two.
%
The first setting is most similar to the situation in Euclidean space studied in Section~\ref{sec:2}: the proofs are almost verbatim the same, with the main difference being a modified radial set since the design illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:radial} is not thin Delone in hyperbolic space.
%
For the second setting, there is an ambiguity in the definition of a $k$-hefty simplex since any $(d-1)$-sphere on the $d$-sphere bounds two complementary $d$-balls.
We thus require that the finite set is \emph{$k$-balanced}, by which we mean that every open hemisphere contains at least $k+1$ points.
With this assumption, only the smaller of the two open $d$-balls has a chance to contain $k$ of the points, so we consider it the inside of the $(d-1)$-sphere.
The proofs of the extensions of Theorems~\ref{thm:covering_number} and \ref{thm:local_covering} to $k$-balanced sets on the sphere are similar to those for thin Delone sets in Euclidean space.
Alternatively, we may use stereographic projection to reduce the spherical to the Euclidean setting.

\smallskip
The generalization to points with real weights is aking to the generalization of Voronoi to power tessellations or diagrams; see e.g.\ \cite[Chapter~13]{Ede87}.
We interpret a point with weight $w \in \Rspace$ as a sphere with squared radius $w$ (which may be negative), and we generalize the circumsphere of a $d$-simplex to the \emph{orthosphere}, which in the generic case is the unique sphere orthogonal to the $d+1$ spheres that are the vertices of the $d$-simplex.
The orthosphere \emph{encloses} a weighted point if the two centers are strictly closer than required for the two spheres to be orthogonal to each other, and the $d$-simplex is \emph{$k$-hefty} if it encloses exactly $k$ of the weighted points.
With these adaptations of the definitions, a claim analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:covering_number} holds also in the weighted case, but Theorem~\ref{thm:local_covering} fails to extend.
Indeed, the $k$-hefty simplices incident to a weighted point cover the neighborhood of the point some integer number of times, but depending on the point and its surrounding, this integer varies between $0$ and $\binom{d+k-1}{d-1}$.


