\subsection{Evaluation of waveguide fundamental solutions}\label{sec:single_q}
There exist many schemes for computing waveguide fundamental solutions, such as Fourier methods based on the Sommerfeld integral formula

In~\cite{goodwill2024numerical}, we built a solver based on the coordinate complexification method, as described in \cite{epstein2024coordinate}. As that method cannot be easily extended to smooth~$q_{l,r}$, in this work we present an evaluator for~$w^{l,r}$ based on Sommerfeld integral formul{\ae}. This solver has the advantage of being simple to implement, even for complex~$x_2,y_2$.

For a given~$q$, the associated~$w$ is the solution of
\begin{equation}
    \Delta w + k^2(1+q) w = -k^2q G_0(\bx,\by). \label{eq:w_eq}
\end{equation}
The derivatives of~$w$ can be evaluated by differentiating the solution with respect to~$\bx$ or the right hand side with respect to~$\by$. 


We build a solver for the more generic 
\begin{equation}
    \Delta u + k^2(1+q) u = -f,
\end{equation}
where the support of~$f$ is contained in the support of~$q$. We write~$u$ in terms of it's Fourier transform in the~$x_1$ variable
\begin{equation}
    u(x_1,x_2) = \int_\gamma \hat u(\xi,x_2)e^{i\xi x_1} {\rm{d}} \xi, \label{eq:u_FT}
\end{equation}
where~$\gamma$ is some yet to be determined contour contained in the second and fourth quadrant

If~$\hat f$ is the Fourier transform of~$f$, then~$\hat u(\xi,\cdot)$ is a bounded solution of:
\begin{equation}
    (-\xi^2 + \partial_{x_2}^2) \hat u + k^2(1+q) \hat u = \hat f,\label{eq:u_tilde}
\end{equation}
for each~$\xi\in\bbC$ that is not a waveguide frequency for~$q$ and not~$\pm k$. Our idea is to numerically solve this equation at quadrature nodes~$\xi_j$ and use the resulting values to evaluate~\eqref{eq:u_FT} numerically.

We solve~\eqref{eq:u_tilde} using a Galerkin method to and write the solution as a linear combination of solutions of the unperturbed ($q=0$) problem. Let~$p_n$ be the~$n$th Legendre polynomial on~$[-d,d]$ and let~$\hat v_n(\xi,\cdot)$ be the bounded solution of
\begin{equation}
    \partial_{x_2}^2 \hat v_n + (k^2-\xi^2)\tilde v_n = \chi_{[-d,d]}(x_2)p_n(x_2),
\end{equation}
where~$\chi_{[-d,d]}$ is the characteristic function of~$[-d,d]$. The Green's function for this problem is well known and allows us to write~$\hat v_n$ as
\begin{equation}
     \hat v_n (\xi; x_2) = -\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{\xi^2 - k^2}} \int_{-d}^d e^{-\sqrt{\xi^2 -k^2} |x_2 -y_2|} p_n (y_2) {\rm d}y_2.
\end{equation}
Using this formula, we can write
\begin{equation}
    \hat v_n(\xi;x_2) = \begin{cases}
        c_{n,\pm}(\xi) e^{-\sqrt{\xi^2 -k^2} |x_2 |} & \pm x_2>d\\
        \sum_{m=0}^\infty G_{nm}(\xi) p_m(x_2) & |x_2|\leq d,
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where~$G_{nm}(\xi)=\int_{-d}^d p_m(x_2) u_n(\xi;x_2)\rm{d}x_2$ and~$c_{n,\pm} = \frac{-1}{2\sqrt{\xi^2-k^2}}\int_{-d}^d e^{\pm \sqrt{\xi^2-k^2}y_2}p_m(x_2) \rm{d}x_2$. Since {\color{red} everything is smooth}, the sum can be truncated with exponential accuracy at~$m=M$. Once all of the coefficients have been computed using standard quadrature rules, this piecewise representation allows for very efficient evaluations of the basis functions~$\hat v_n$. We now return to finding a basis for the solutions of the perturbed problem.

The basis for the perturbed problem is the bounded solutions of
\begin{equation}
    \partial_{x_2}^2 \hat u_n + (k^2-\xi^2)\hat u_n = \chi_{[-d,d]}(x_2)p_n(x_2) - k^2q \hat u_n
\end{equation}
for each~$n$. Since~$\hat u_n$ is the solution of~\eqref{eq:u_tilde} with a right hand side supported in~$[-d,d]$, it can be written as a linear combination of~$\hat v_n$'s:
\begin{equation}
    \hat u_n(\xi,\cdot) = \sum_{m=0}^\infty\tilde G_{nm}(\xi) \hat v_n(\xi,\cdot).\label{eq:u_exp}
\end{equation}
