\section{Introduction}

Answering path queries is a fundamental operation on graphs, with numerous optimization and information retrieval tasks relying on an efficient response to such queries.
Bacic et al.~\cite{Bacic_beer_path} recently introduced the concepts of \emph{beer vertices} and \emph{beer paths} to model the option of taking a detour towards a set of \emph{points of interest} when travelling from a source point to a destination point.
In fact, choosing detours is an essential part of daily life and modern computing systems. When travelling from point A to point B, we often need to adjust our route, perhaps to refuel at a gas station or pick up a beer.
Given a weighted graph $G=(V,E,w)$ and a subset $B \subseteq V$ of beer vertices, a beer path
between two vertices $u$ and $v$ is a path that starts from $u$, ends at $v$, and visits at least one beer vertex
in $B$. The \emph{beer distance} between two vertices is the shortest length of any beer path connecting them.

Since their introduction, beer paths and distances have been extensively studied in different graph classes and frameworks~\cite{Bacic_beer_path, bilo_spanner_beer,Coudert_beer_path_indexing,Das_beer_path_interval, Gudmundsson_beer_path_treewidth, Hanaka_beer_path_decomposition}.
All existing techniques, however, are applicable to \textit{static} graphs, which limits the uses of beer paths. In fact, because of the inherent nature of beer path applications, temporal information is a fundamental feature to consider in such scenarios. For example, bus routes may operate only at specific times, while beer shops may be subject to opening and closing hours.
Temporal graphs~\cite{hosseinzadeh2022temporal,kostakos_temporal_graphs,Michail_temporal_graphs,zhang2024efficient} extend the concept of static graphs to include such temporal information, meaning that each edge is associated with a starting time and a travelling time, and two vertices are adjacent only in the time instants in which an edge incident to them exists. 
In this work we introduce and study beer path problems in temporal graphs.

