\subsection{Our contribution}
In this paper, we introduce the concept of beer paths in temporal graphs and several related optimization problems, namely \emph{earliest-arrival}, \emph{latest-departure}, \emph{fastest}, and \emph{shortest temporal beer paths} (in short EABP, LDBP, FBP, SBP). %\AnnaComment{maybe ETBP, LTBP, FTBP, STBP with T from temporal} \SotirisComment{We usually skip the word temporal throughout the paper}
In order to further incorporate temporal information that more accurately reflects real-life applications, our framework also considers timestamped beer vertices, i.e., the case where each beer vertex is augmented with the time instants\footnote{We consider time instants and not intervals. With slight modifications, our algorithms can work for intervals as well, with logarithmic dependence on the number of intervals.} in which it is active as a point of interest.
This feature naturally models many scenarios, e.g., opening and closing times of bars or gas stations; active motorway toll booths; instants in which airports may serve for emergency landing, and so on. 

%\SotirisComment{I propose the following to shorten this part. Makes it about 1/4 page shorter.}

In Section~\ref{sec:algo}, we propose efficient algorithms for the aforementioned objectives, with each one differing both in methods and in time complexity. We define multiple-source earliest-arrival paths (MSEAP) and multiple-target latest-departure paths (MTLDP) and propose algorithms for calculating them, as an intermediate step for EABP and LDBP.\footnote{MSEAPs and MTLDPs have interesting applications in their own right; for example, a MSEAP is the optimal path for sending an ambulance to some target destination, assuming an ambulance can start from multiple sources (hospitals), at a different time for each source.} Using MSEAP subroutines, we propose two algorithms for EABP, one for the edge stream model and one for the adjacency list model, with the second one being significantly faster for graphs with no dominated edges. Similarly, we propose two algorithms for LDBP based on MTLDP subroutines. For FBP and SBP we rely on the computation of certain non-dominated paths via various domination criteria. For SBP in particular, we define a new path domination criterion and propose an algorithm for computing respective paths. The time complexity of our algorithms generally aligns with that of respective temporal path algorithms.

In Section~\ref{sec:preprocessing}, we propose preprocessing methods for answering beer path queries faster. For EABP and LDBP, this is achieved through the precomputation of selected non-dominated paths in the graph. For FBP and SBP, we utilize the graph transformation proposed by~\cite{Path_Wu}. For FBP, this offers a speedup compared to the algorithm of Section~\ref{sec:algo}. For SBP, no speedup is achieved, but we obtain a one-to-all SBP algorithm, which is significant because the respective algorithm of Section~\ref{sec:algo} only runs for a specific source and target.


