\subtitle{Perceptual loss with ray-based regularization and coarse-to-fine optimization.}
Previous point-to-shape auto-encoders rely solely on occupancy, which results in poor performance when reconstructing high-frequency details. To address this, we propose enhancing the auto-encoder using normal maps with perceptual loss as additional supervision. During training, we query the occupancy of a \(256^3\) grid and extract the iso-surface differentiably~\citep{wei2023neumanifold}. 
% We compute the render loss using nvdiffrast~\citep{Laine2020diffrast} to differentiably rasterize the normal map.
However, early experiments show that simply incorporating render loss will cause severe floaters in the final output mesh (see \aref{app:more_ablations}), a problem also noted in previous research~\citep{wei2024meshlrm}. To mitigate this, we propose a ray-based regularization that forces the occupancy in empty spaces to approach zero. As illustrated in the left part of \fref{fig:augmentation}, for each camera ray, we uniformly sample \(N_s\) points between the ray-bounding box intersection and the surface point, enforcing their occupancy to be near zero. 
% To save GPU VRAM and accelerate training, the occupancy values are interpolated from previously obtained occupancy grid, rather than querying the triplane.
Due to the locality of differentiable marching cubes, the gradients of the render loss can only propagate to points near surface vertices. Therefore, a coarse-to-fine training process is necessary to ensure the effectiveness of the render loss. During the coarse stage, we apply standard binary cross-entropy (BCE) loss for the point-to-shape auto-encoder, along with a KL loss to regularize the latent space and a total variation loss~\citep{yu2021plenoxels} to reduce floaters, i.e.

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{coarse}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}} + \lambda_{\text{KL}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} + \lambda_{\text{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{TV}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}$, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{TV}}$ denote the BCE loss, the KL loss and the total variation loss respectively, $\lambda_{\text{KL}}$ and $\lambda_{\text{TV}}$ refer to the loss weights.
After the coarse stage training, the model is capable of reconstructing a coarse mesh from the input point cloud. In the refinement stage, we exploit render loss with ray-based regularization to enhance the details of the reconstructed mesh:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        \mathcal{L}_{\text{refine}}=&\mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}} + \lambda_{\text{KL}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}} + \lambda_{\text{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{TV}} + 
        \lambda_{\text{MSE}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{normal}}^{\text{MSE}}\\
        &+\lambda_{\text{LPIPS}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{normal}}^{\text{LPIPS}} +
        \lambda_{\text{reg}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{reg}},
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{normal}}^{\text{MSE}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{normal}}^{\text{LPIPS}}$ denotes the MSE and LPIPS~\citep{LPIPS} loss for rendered normal, $\lambda_{\text{MSE}}$, $\lambda_{\text{LPIPS}}$, and $\lambda_{\text{reg}}$ refers to the corresponding loss weight. The concrete hyper-parameter settings are presented in \aref{app:details}.

\subsection{Image-to-shape diffusion model with generative data augmentation}
\label{sec:diffusion}
% \subtitle{Generative data augmentation.}
As shown in \fref{fig:comp_lrm}, compared to large reconstruction models, existing native 3D generation models tend to generate simple and symmetrical shapes that differ significantly from the reference images.
We believe this phenomenon arises because the training of existing models predominantly relies on the Objaverse dataset~\citep{objaverse}, and a considerable portion of the objects in Objaverse have symmetrical geometry and lack realistic textures. Therefore, the diffusion models trained on it tend to replicate simple geometries and struggle to generalize to images with complex textures or lighting.
% \shit{As is well known, training a promising and generalizable diffusion model, such as Stable Diffusion models that convert text into images, requires a substantial amount of data~\citep{stablediffusion, SDXL}. Typically, hundreds of millions of text-image pairs are required to achieve satisfactory performance. In contrast, 3D data presents unique challenges due to the complexity of its creation, the specialized skills required, and issues surrounding copyright, which result in far fewer publicly accessible datasets compared to image or video data.}
% 为了能够在有限的数据上训练出泛化能力强的diffusion模型，我们注意到了shapegen这种pipeline相比于lrm/3dtopia这种pipeline的两个关键不同点：（1）我们的auto-encoder相比于3dtopia这种需要逐个物体training的相比更加灵活，可以根据输入点云进行on-the-fly的计算；（2）之前的方法是建模image-to-textured mesh，这导致input image和输出的mesh的纹理是entangle的，而我们的diffusion只关注于对image-to-shape mapping的建模，这意味着，对于同一个shape，其在不同纹理或光照条件下的渲染图，都应该对应于同一个shape。基于这两点观察，我们提出了对于train image-to-shape至关重要的两个data augmentation.
To obtain a diffusion model with strong generalization capabilities on limited public data, we identify two key differences between the point-to-shape auto-encoder based and the previous NeRF-based native 3D generation pipeline (typical methods include Rodin~\citep{Wang2023rodin}, 3DTopia~\citep{hong20243dtopia}, etc):
% 这不仅需要提前计算和储存大量latent vectors，并且无法灵活地应对物体的旋转，因为旋转之后需要重新optimize nerf来获得triplane隐变量(请注意，通过相应旋转采样点的方式虽然可以得到旋转后的渲染图像，不同旋转还是对应了同样一组latent vector)，而point-to-shape auto-encoder则可以通过旋转对应的点云来实现物体的旋转。
\subtitle{(1) Geometrical covariant auto-encoder.} 
Previous NeRF-based native 3D generation methods utilize a per-object optimized NeRF as the latent representation, which requires pre-computing and storing numerous latents and lacks geometric covariance, as it necessitates re-optimizing the radiance field to obtain triplane latent variables after applying a transformation to the object. In contrast, the point-to-shape auto-encoder takes point clouds as input and does not require per-object optimization, it naturally achieves geometric covariance for transformations such as rotations by directly manipulating the point cloud.
% When a rotation is applied to an object, the corresponding radiance field needs to be re-optimized to fit the rotated views. It is important to note that despite one can obtain the rotated rendered images with the original radiance field by rotating the ray samples, the underlying latent remains the same, which means no diversity is gained during the diffusion training. In contrast, the point-to-shape auto-encoder can achieve object rotation by manipulating the input point cloud accordingly.
\subtitle{(2) Appearance invariant image-to-shape modeling.} 
% Previous methods learn to generate textured assets, resulting in the entanglement of input images and the textures. In contrast, our diffusion model is designed to map images to shapes only, which means for the same shape, renderings under any reasonable texture or lighting conditions correspond to the same shape. 
Previous NeRF-based methods generate textured assets, causing entanglement between input images and textures. In contrast, our image-to-shape diffusion model maps images solely to shapes, meaning renderings under reasonable texture or lighting conditions should correspond to the same shape.

Based on both insights, we propose two data augmentations that are critical for training the image-to-shape model.
% 为了充分利用现有的publicly available数据集中包含的多视角的渲染图(例如gobjaverse)，我们结合point-to-shape auto-encoder可以灵活应对旋转点云的特性，提出了3D rotation augmentaion。具体来说，对于数据集中的物体，每次训练的时候选取其多视角图片中的一个视角，然后旋转点云的azimuth，使得该物体的朝向和选取的图像相同，将这样匹配的图像-shape pair作为diffusion模型训练的数据。我们的实验表明，这种rotation augmentation不仅可以扩充训练时的数据集大小，并且，将物体旋转至和图像相同azimuth的操作极大地提升了生成shape和图像的匹配程度，克服了之前方法（例如craftsman倾向于生成对称物体的问题）。我们把这个效果归功于，由于objaverse中的物体有相当一部分是对称的，如果不加入旋转augmentation，模型可能会过分关注对称性而忽略与图像之间的对应关系，旋转使得模型可以更加关注到图像和shape之间的对应关系，进而使得生成的shape和原图更为相似，这为后续的texturing提供了一定程度的便利

\subtitle{Geometric alignment augmentation.}
% To enhance the diffusion model's learning of image-shape correspondence, we propose utilizing the properties of a point-to-shape autoencoder to ensure that different views of the same object correspond to different latents.
% To fully utilize the multi-view renderings included in current datasets (such as GObjaverse~\citep{qiu2024richdreamer}), we propose a rotation augmentation metho that combines a point-to-shape autoencoder, which can flexibly handle the characteristics of rotating point clouds.
To enhance image-shape correspondence during training, we propose utilizing the geometric covariance property of our point-to-shape auto-encoder to ensure that different views of the same object correspond to different latents.
Specifically, for each object in the dataset, we select one view from multi-view images as the condition and rotate the point cloud's azimuth to align the object's orientation with the selected image as the target (see the middle part of~\fref{fig:augmentation} for a simple demonstration).
The aligned image-shape pairs are then used as training data for the diffusion model.
Our experiments reveal that geometric alignment not only expands the training dataset but also significantly improves the alignment between generated shapes and images. We present the corresponding ablation study in ~\fref{fig:ablation}.
% The alignment between meshes and images in the training dataset addresses limitations of previous methods, such as CraftSMAN's tendency to produce symmetric objects, which arises because many Objaverse objects are symmetric. Without rotation augmentation, the model risks overemphasizing symmetry at the expense of image-shape correspondence. By incorporating rotations, the model enhances this correspondence, resulting in shapes that closely resemble the original images, and facilitating subsequent texturing.

% （2）generative rendering augmentation。 基于之前的分析，我们的diffusion建模的目标和mesh的纹理无关，因此数据集中的mesh在任何合理的纹理和光照条件下的渲染图都应该得到完全相同的shape。Leveraging this insight,我们提出使用controlnet和relightning diffusion模型对于数据集中的渲染图进行augmentation。具体来说，对于数据集中的每一张渲染图，我们利用数据集中的normal map和depth map作为controlnet的条件，为了能够让augmantation之后的图像和原图不偏离太多，我们利用ip adapter注入原始图像的信息，并且令controlnet从原始图像的noisy version开始denoise。我们在\fref{}中展示了一些generative rendering augmentation的一些生成结果，我们提出的方法很大程度上弥补了objaverse数据集中材质较为简单的问题。除此之外，我们提出使用IC-lights来丰富渲染图中的光照条件。
\subtitle{Generative rendering augmentation.}
To enhance the generalization ability of the image-to-shape diffusion, we propose leveraging the appearance-invariant property by utilizing generative rendering to synthesize images with realistic textures and rich lighting based on the geometry of the object.
% Based on previous analyses, our diffusion modeling goals are independent of the mesh texture. Therefore, the rendered images of the meshes in the dataset should yield the same shape under any reasonable texture and lighting conditions. 
% Concretely, we exploit ControlNet~\citep{zhang2023adding} for realistic texture generation and IC-light~\citep{iclight} for generating renderings under different lighting condition. 
Concretely, for each rendered image in the dataset, we utilize the corresponding normal map and depth map as control signals to synthesize realistic renderings with ControlNet~\citep{zhang2023adding}. 
% To ensure that the augmented images do not deviate significantly from the originals, we inject the original image using an IP-adapter~\citep{ye2023ip-adapter}. 
We then use IC-light~\citep{iclight} to generate renderings under various lighting conditions. Experiments show that generative rendering augmentation is highly beneficial for helping diffusion models understand lighting effects and generalize to real-world images (see \fref{fig:ablation} for the corresponding ablation study).
% In the right part of \fref{fig:augmentation}, we present some generated results from our generative rendering augmentation, and our proposed method largely compensates for the issue of simpler materials in the Objaverse dataset.
% We demonstrate some samples generated by our generative data augmentation pipeline in \fref{fig:genaug}.

% 我们使用了一个和stable diffusion相似的unet结构作为image-to-shape的denoise模型。We follow RODIN，将triplanes在height维度concat起来作为unet的输入来避免channel上concat造成的空间不匹配，不同plane之间的则通过self-attention进行交互。为了在diffusion的过程中加入图像的信息，我们使用了DINOv2对输入图像进行编码，然后通过cross-attention的方式将图像信息注入到denoise过程中。Following SD3，我们使用了rectified flow作为。更多关于我们diffusion unet的训练和推理细节请参考\aref{}。
\subtitle{Image-to-3D diffusion UNet.} 
% We adopt a UNet similar to Stable Diffusion~\citep{stablediffusion} as the image-to-shape diffusion network. Following Rodin~\citep{Wang2023rodin}, we concatenate triplanes along the height dimension as input to the UNet to avoid spatial mismatches. Interactions between different planes are handled via self-attention layers. To incorporate image information during diffusion, we encode the input image using DINOv2~\citep{oquab2024dinov} and inject the extracted features into the denoising process through cross-attention. Following SD3~\citep{esser2024scaling}, we adopt rectified flow~\cite{liu2022flow} with lognorm timestep sampling as the training schedule. For more details on the training and inference of our diffusion UNet, please refer to \aref{app:details}.
Based on the proposed two augmentations, we train a single-image-to-shape diffusion UNet on a filtered subset of GObjaverse~\citep{qiu2024richdreamer}. 
Our diffusion UNet is similar to Stable Diffusion~\citep{stablediffusion}, consisting of interleaved convolution and attention layers.
Following Rodin~\citep{Wang2023rodin}, we concatenate triplanes along the height dimension as input to the UNet to avoid spatial mismatches. Interactions between different planes are handled via self-attention layers. 
% To incorporate image information during diffusion, we encode the input image using DINOv2~\citep{oquab2024dinov} and inject the extracted features into the denoising process through cross-attention. 
The input reference image is encoded using pre-trained DINOv2~\citep{oquab2024dinov} and injected with cross-attention.
Following SD3~\citep{esser2024scaling}, we adopt rectified flow~\cite{liu2022flow} with lognorm timestep sampling as the training schedule. For more details on the training and inference of our diffusion UNet, please refer to \aref{app:details}.

\subsection{Texture Generation}
\label{sec:texture}
Our texturing pipeline starts by generating multi-view shaded images using a geometry-conditioned ControlNet with reference attention. The corresponding PBR components are estimated using a PBR decomposer, then back-projected to UV space and inpainted to fill invisible areas. The detailed pipeline is as follows.

